(window.MUSEUM_MATCH_T = window.MUSEUM_MATCH_T || {})["en"] = {"ui":{"lead":"Great games, one move at a time. Follow famous games on the board while Musowl explains each move.","all":"All","k_chess":"Chess","k_shogi":"Shogi","k_go":"Go","k_xiangqi":"Xiangqi","side_r":"Red","res_r":"Red won","back":"Back to Great Games","start":"Start","prev":"Back one move","next":"Forward one move","end":"End","play":"Play","pause":"Pause","moves":"Moves","move":"Move {n}","startpos":"Start","nextnote":"Next note (move {n})","quiet":"No note for this move.","guide":"Musowl","keys":"← → step through the moves","result":"Result","r_event":"Game","r_date":"Year","r_place":"Place","side_w":"White","side_b":"Black","side_s":"Sente","side_g":"Gote","side_B":"Black","side_W":"White","res_w":"White won","res_b":"Black won","res_d":"Draw","res_s":"Sente won","res_g":"Gote won","credit":"The moves are the public record of each game. The notes are by the museum’s Musowl.","caps":"Captured","hand":"In hand","pnote":"Previous note","nnote":"Next note","plies":"{n} moves","about":"About this game","ev":"Evaluation","even":"Even","evby":"Read by this site’s engine (a rough guide): the first player’s chance of winning","r_dp":"When & where"},"opera":{"t":"The Opera Game","w":"Paul Morphy","b":"Duke Karl II of Brunswick and Count Isouard","ev":"Played in a box at the Paris Opera (the two allies consulting)","d":"1858","pl":"France (Paris)","lead":"The young American champion against two noblemen, during a performance at the opera: seventeen moves. Bring the pieces out fast, give them up without a second thought, and finish with very few. No game appears in more chess books.","c":{"0":"Paris, 1858. Twenty-one-year-old American Paul Morphy had been beating Europe's strong players one after another. That evening, in a box at the opera, the Duke of Brunswick and Count Isouard took Black together and played him, talking over each move. It is said Morphy wanted to finish quickly so he could watch the stage. These seventeen moves are full of the basics: bring pieces out fast, keep your king safe.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares. It eyes d5 and e5 in the centre and opens paths for the bishop and queen.","2":"Black also plays e5. The pawns face each other in the centre, and White's pawn cannot go further.","3":"The knight comes to f3 and attacks the e5 pawn. A good move: it develops a piece and makes Black think about defending.","4":"Black guards e5 with d6. This is called the Philidor Defence. It is solid, but it makes the path of the bishop on f8 a little narrow.","5":"White plays d4, bringing another pawn to the centre to hit e5. Black must decide whether to take or defend.","6":"Black's bishop goes to g4. It presses the knight on f3, which stands in line with White's queen on d1. If the knight moves, the queen can be taken, so the knight cannot go after e5.","7":"White takes on e5 with the pawn. If Black took back at once with the d6 pawn, White would trade queens and then the knight would grab the e5 pawn. So Black first wants to get rid of the knight on f3.","8":"Black takes the knight with the bishop. Now e5 can be won back safely. But giving up the bishop that had just come out for the knight leaves Black behind in development.","9":"White takes back with the queen. White's queen is already working near the middle, while Black has hardly brought out any pieces.","10":"Black takes back with a pawn, and the material is even. But White is ahead in pieces that are out and ready.","11":"Bishop to c4. Together with the queen on f3 it aims at f7. Only the king guards f7, so it is a weak square; taking it would be mate.","12":"Black brings the knight to f6 and blocks the f-file. Without this, queen takes f7 would be mate. Defending while developing is the right answer.","13":"The queen swings to b3 and attacks f7 and b7 at the same time. A move with two threats is strong, because the opponent cannot defend both.","14":"Black plays the queen to e7 to guard f7, letting b7 go. But the queen now blocks the bishop on f8.","15":"Morphy does not take the pawn on b7; he develops a knight instead. Taking would send the queen far away. Choosing fast development over a pawn is typical of Morphy.","16":"Black plays c6, covering d5 and b5 and opening a road for the queen to b6.","17":"Bishop to g5 pins the knight on f6. If the knight moves, the queen on e7 behind it can be taken. Black's pieces find it harder and harder to move.","18":"Black plays b5 to chase the bishop from c4. It also gains room on the queen's side, but it opens a gap for White's pieces to jump in.","19":"The knight is given up to take on b5! Instead of retreating the bishop, Morphy keeps attacking. With Black's king still in the middle, this is very dangerous for Black.","20":"Black takes the knight and is a knight ahead. But Black's king is still on e8, and White's pieces keep gathering.","21":"Bishop to b5, check! Black's king is still stuck in the middle. White has two pawns for the knight and comes straight at the king.","22":"Black blocks with the knight on d7. But this knight is pinned: if it moves, the black king is in check. White will now aim at d7.","23":"White castles queenside. The king is safe, and at the same time the rook arrives on d1 to aim at d7. One move does both defence and attack: a superb move.","24":"Black brings the rook to d8 to guard d7. Black still cannot castle, and the rook on h8 and bishop on f8 are doing nothing.","25":"The rook is given up to take on d7! White hands over one rook to remove one of Black's defenders.","26":"Black takes back with the rook. Now this rook on d7 is pinned too: if it moves, the bishop on b5 checks the black king.","27":"The other rook comes to d1 and attacks d7 again. Now more white pieces attack d7 than black pieces defend it.","28":"Black's queen goes to e6, offering a queen trade to push White's queen away from b3, and hoping to help d7. But it is too late.","29":"The bishop takes on d7 with check. White removes Black's defenders one by one.","30":"Black takes back with the knight. With the knight gone from f6, the diagonal from the bishop on g5 to d8 is open. That diagonal will matter at the very end.","31":"The queen is given up on b8, with check! This is the most famous move of the game. Black must take with the knight, and when the knight leaves d7 the d-file opens.","32":"Black takes with the knight. Now no black piece guards d8.","33":"Rook to d8, checkmate! The bishop on g5 supports d8, and the black king is boxed in by its own pieces. White gave away queen, rook and bishop, and finished with just a rook and a bishop. Develop quickly, keep your king safe, and attack while the enemy king is stuck in the middle: this famous game shows all these important ideas."}},"reti1910":{"t":"Réti's Mate","w":"Richard Réti","b":"Savielly Tartakower","ev":"A casual game (not a tournament)","d":"1910","pl":"Austria (Vienna)","lead":"Two future grandmasters, a casual game in Vienna, eleven moves. White gives up the queen and finishes with a double check: two pieces checking at once. The mating pattern is named after White: Réti's Mate.","c":{"0":"Vienna, 1910. White is twenty-year-old Richard Réti; Black is Savielly Tartakower. Both went on to become top players and are known for bringing new ideas to chess. This was not a tournament game but a casual one, played for a small stake. It is short, but it shows clearly why you should get your king safe early.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares. Taking the middle of the board makes it easier for the pieces behind to come out. The bishop and queen now have open paths.","2":"Black plays c6. Instead of going to the centre at once, Black gets ready for d5 to hit the pawn on e4. This solid opening is called the Caro-Kann Defence.","3":"White puts the other pawn on d4 too. Two pawns stand side by side in the centre, and White has plenty of space. Black will try to break them up.","4":"As planned, d5. The pawn challenges e4: \"I can take you.\" White must decide whether to guard it, push it or trade it.","5":"White brings out a knight to guard e4. It develops a piece and defends at the same time, so no time is wasted.","6":"Black takes the pawn on e4. By trading centre pawns, Black makes White's centre smaller.","7":"White takes back with the knight, which now stands in the middle of the board. A central knight reaches many squares and is a strong piece.","8":"Black brings out a knight to face the one on e4. Bishop to f5 is often played here, but Black invites a knight trade.","9":"The queen comes out early to d3 to guard the knight on e4. Usually you should not bring the queen out too soon, but White has a plan. Remember that this queen stands on the d-file, in front of Black's king.","10":"Black plays e5, offering a pawn to open lines for the bishop and queen. It is an active move that starts a fight in the centre.","11":"White takes the pawn on e5. White accepts the gift, but this also gives Black's queen more room to move.","12":"Check! Black's queen on a5 aims diagonally at the king on e1. At the same time it plans to win back the e5 pawn: two jobs in one move. White must block.","13":"White blocks the check with the bishop on d2. The queen could take the bishop, but White's queen guards it well.","14":"Black wins the pawn back, so material is equal again. And this queen looks straight down the e-file at the knight on e4 and the king behind it. If the knight moves, the king is in check, so the knight is stuck. Black seems about to win it.","15":"White castles queenside. The king leaves the e-file, so the knight is free again. At the same time a rook comes to d1: the rook on d1, the queen on d3 and the square d8 next to Black's king are all on one line. This is White's trap.","16":"Black takes the knight on e4 and seems to win a piece. But Black's king is still on e8 and has not castled. White's pieces are lined up on the d-file, and the black king is unprotected. This move, which loses the game, is the turning point.","17":"Queen to d8, check! White gives away the strongest piece for nothing. The only black piece that can take it is the king. By letting it be taken, White drags the king onto the dangerous square d8.","18":"Black must take with the king; there is no other way to stop the check. The king is pulled out to d8, with the rook on d1 waiting behind.","19":"The bishop moves from d2 to g5 with check, and at the same time it opens the way for the rook on d1, which checks along the d-file too. Two pieces checking at once is a double check. You cannot stop it by taking one piece or by blocking, so the king must run.","20":"The king runs to c7. If it went to e8 instead, the rook would come to d8 and mate. Wherever the king goes, White has a finishing move ready.","21":"Bishop to d8: checkmate on the diagonal against the king on c7! The bishop is guarded by the rook on d1, so the king cannot take it. Squares like b8, c8, b7 and c6 are blocked by Black's own pieces, and the rook watches d6 and d7. In just eleven moves, White gave up the queen and won with two small pieces. The game shows how dangerous it is to grab material before castling. Some records say Black resigned one move earlier."}},"immortal":{"t":"The Immortal Game","w":"Adolf Anderssen","b":"Lionel Kieseritzky","ev":"An informal game during a break in the London 1851 tournament","d":"1851","pl":"United Kingdom (London)","lead":"London, during the first international chess tournament: a game played in a break. White gives up a bishop, both rooks and finally the queen, and mates with the three small pieces left. Black is said to have resigned before the end; the last few moves may be the finish White showed.","c":{"0":"London, June 1851. That year the world’s first international chess tournament was being held in London. White is Adolf Anderssen, a mathematics teacher from Germany; Black is Lionel Kieseritzky, who taught chess at the Café de la Régence in Paris. This was not a tournament game but a relaxed game played during a break. Kieseritzky printed the moves in his own magazine and spread them, and the game later came to be called “the Immortal Game”. Let’s watch a battle typical of its time, full of pieces thrown into the attack. Musowl will be your guide.","1":"White pushes the pawn in front of the king two squares. It now watches the central squares d5 and f5, and the paths for the bishop and queen are open. Then as now, it is the most popular first move.","2":"Black also plays e5. The pawns meet head-on and Black does not give up the centre. Black’s bishop and queen now have open paths too.","3":"f4! White offers a pawn for free: the “King’s Gambit”. If Black takes it, Black’s e5 pawn leaves the centre and White can more easily push a pawn to d4 as well. In this era, plans like this that rush into attack were hugely popular. The weak point is that the space in front of White’s king is opened up.","4":"Black accepts the pawn. The pawn on f4 blocks the road of White’s bishop on c1 and also watches the squares e3 and g3. Black is a pawn ahead.","5":"White brings the bishop out to c4. The square f7 next to Black’s king is weak, guarded only by the king. White aims at it so an attack can start at any moment.","6":"Check! Because White pushed the f-pawn, a diagonal road from h4 to e1 was open. If White blocks with g3, the pawn on f4 simply takes it. White has to move the king.","7":"The king steps aside to f1. Now White can no longer castle and cannot tuck the king away safely. On the other hand, Black’s queen has come out early, so White can now chase it while bringing out pieces.","8":"Black offers a pawn for free too. With b5 Black lures the c4 bishop away from the diagonal that aims at f7. This move was later named the “Bryan Countergambit”, and it was an attacking move Kieseritzky liked to use.","9":"White takes the pawn. The bishop has left c4, so it no longer aims at f7. In return White has won a pawn back, and the count is even again.","10":"Black’s knight goes to f6. It attacks the e4 pawn and also helps guard the king, a fine square. The race to bring out pieces has begun.","11":"White’s knight comes out to f3 as well. It develops a piece and threatens the queen on h4 at the same time, a very satisfying move. The queen has to run, so White has effectively gained a move.","12":"The queen drops back to h6. From here it watches the h-file and sees the rook on h1 behind White’s h2 pawn. It is still a safe square.","13":"With d3, White firmly guards the e4 pawn that was under attack. At the same time the road for the c1 bishop opens. A calm move that wastes nothing.","14":"Black’s knight goes to h5. It guards the f4 pawn and plans to jump to g3. A knight jump to g3 would give check and hit the h1 rook; if White takes with the h2 pawn, the h-file opens and the queen on h6 can capture the h1 rook. A rather sharp plan.","15":"White’s knight goes to h4. This knight blocks the h-file, so even if Black’s knight jumps to g3 the h1 rook can no longer be taken. It also prepares to jump to f5 and threaten the queen on h6.","16":"Black’s queen goes to g5, attacking the knight on h4. White has to decide whether to defend the knight or move it somewhere.","17":"The knight jumps to f5. Protected by the e4 pawn, it is a very strong post. From here it attacks the g7 pawn and keeps an eye on the squares h6 and d6. White’s pieces are closing in on Black’s king.","18":"With c6 Black tries to chase away the bishop on b5. If the bishop runs, Black can grow the centre with pawns. A normal, sensible move.","19":"But White does not save the bishop and plays g4!, threatening the knight on h5. White is willing to lose a bishop in order to chase Black’s pieces around and gain time for the attack: a bold idea. The g4 pawn is protected diagonally by the queen on d1.","20":"The knight, attacked on h5, goes back to f6. Black decides that taking White’s bishop can wait. The c6 pawn is still attacking the bishop on b5.","21":"The rook goes to g1. It guards the g4 pawn and gets ready to fight on the g-file. The bishop on b5 is still left to be taken. White believes that chasing Black’s queen matters more than one bishop.","22":"Black takes the bishop. In material, Black is now a whole bishop ahead, a big lead. But apart from the queen and a knight, Black’s pieces are still on their starting squares. White’s attack starts from here.","23":"h4! White chases Black’s queen on g5 with a pawn. Just by pushing pawns, White keeps shrinking the places where Black’s queen can stand.","24":"The queen escapes to g6. It still has some squares, but it is starting to be surrounded by White’s pawns and knight.","25":"With h5 White chases the queen once more. Every time a White pawn advances, Black’s queen can only retreat.","26":"The queen goes back to g5. The knight on f5 watches h6 and the other squares are blocked, so this is almost the only place left. Black’s queen is slowly being cornered.","27":"White’s queen goes to f3. Next, if the c1 bishop takes the f4 pawn, the queen on g5 will be attacked and have almost nowhere to run. It is a move that sets a trap to catch the queen.","28":"Black brings the knight back to g8 to free the escape square f6 for the queen. A knight that was developed returning to its starting square is a sign that Black is in trouble. In the race to bring out pieces, White is far ahead.","29":"The bishop captures the f4 pawn, winning a pawn back while chasing the queen on g5. White’s bishop has finally joined the fight as well.","30":"The queen escapes to the square it prepared, f6. From here it looks diagonally all the way to the b2 pawn and can also aim at the a1 rook behind it.","31":"White’s knight goes to c3. The knight on c3 also blocks the diagonal from the f6 queen to b2. Next it plans to jump to d5 and threaten the queen on f6.","32":"Black brings the bishop out to c5 and attacks the rook on g1. At last Black has brought out a bishop and joined the attack. Should Black go grab pieces, or defend the king? It was an important moment for Black.","33":"The knight goes to d5! It threatens the f6 queen, and there is also a jump to c7 that would attack the king and rook at once. But because the knight left c3, the road from Black’s queen to b2 is open again. White does not try to protect either the g1 rook or the b2 pawn. White is ready to let pieces be taken in order to attack Black’s king.","34":"Black’s queen takes the b2 pawn and now aims at the a1 rook. The idea is to grab lots of pieces and win on numbers. But the queen is getting farther and farther away from its own king.","35":"The bishop goes to d6! Now White leaves both rooks to be taken. In return the bishop controls f8 and e7, blocking the escape routes of Black’s king. White chose not the piece count but weaving a net to catch Black’s king. This is the turning point of the game.","36":"Black takes the rook on g1. Black is now ahead by a bishop and a rook. However, later studies say another move was better here, and that Black had a stronger defence.","37":"e5! A quiet pawn move, but it is one of the deciding blows. This pawn blocks the diagonal from the b2 queen to g7 and also controls f6. Black’s queen can no longer come back to help the king, and the g8 knight cannot come out to f6. White now threatens to take on g7 with the knight, giving check, and then mate with the bishop on c7.","38":"Black takes the other rook too, with check. The whole first rank from a1 to f1 was open. Black has taken both rooks and is far ahead in material.","39":"The king steps to e2. White’s only big piece left is the queen, plus two knights and a bishop. Even so, around Black’s king White has far more pieces than Black.","40":"Black brings the knight out to a6 to guard c7. It is meant to stop the mate where the knight takes g7 with check, the king runs to d8, and the bishop finishes on c7. It is said that Kieseritzky resigned around here, and that the rest of the moves were shown by White.","41":"The knight takes the g7 pawn with check! The knight on g7 attacks the king on e8. The bishop on d6 controls e7 and f8, so the king has few places to go.","42":"The king has to run to d8. It can no longer stay on e8 or go to e7 or f8; d8 was the only square left.","43":"White sacrifices the queen on f6 with check! Even the last big piece is given away for free. It checks diagonally from f6 through e7 to d8, and Black has no choice but to take this queen.","44":"Black takes the queen with the g8 knight. The king has nowhere to run, so there is no other move. But this knight moving away completes the mating pattern.","45":"The bishop goes to e7. Checkmate! The knight on d5 controls e7 and c7, the knight on g7 controls e8, and Black’s own pieces block c8 and d7. White gave up a bishop, two rooks and even the queen, and won with just three small pieces. Black still has a queen and rooks to spare, but none of them arrive in time. It teaches us that how hard your pieces are working matters more than how many you have, and it is one of the most famous games in the history of chess. The name is said to have been given a few years later by the Austrian master Falkbeer."}},"mimiaka":{"t":"The Ear-reddening Game","w":"Honinbo Shusaku","b":"Inoue Gennan Inseki","ev":"A game in Osaka (played over three days)","d":"1846","pl":"Japan (Osaka)","lead":"Seventeen-year-old Shusaku (4-dan) took on Gennan (8-dan), one of the strongest players of the day. When Shusaku played move 127, Gennan’s ears are said to have turned red — hence the name. There was no komi then; Black won by 2 points.","c":{"0":"Osaka, 1846. Black is 17-year-old Shusaku (4-dan); White is Inoue Gennan Inseki (8-dan), about 48, one of the top players of the day who had once fought Honinbo Jowa for the post of Meijin Godokoro. The day before, Shusaku had started with two handicap stones, as their ranks required, but Gennan soon saw his strength and stopped that game; this one was played with Shusaku simply taking Black. There was no komi in those days, so only the territory on the board counts. Let us follow this three-day game move by move.","1":"Black 1 is on the 3-4 point at top right (R16). It balances corner territory and room to extend along the sides. Shusaku liked 3-4 points when he had Black.","2":"White 2 takes the 3-4 point at top left (D17). In the opening the corners come first, because territory is easiest to make there. The two sides share out the empty corners.","3":"Black 3 is the 3-4 point at bottom right (Q3). It is lined up with R16 so that the right side between the two corners can later become a big Black area.","4":"White 4 (P17) approaches Black’s top-right corner. If Black got one more move there, the corner would become very solid, so White acts first. Black can answer, or play another big point instead.","5":"Black 5 does not answer but takes the 3-4 point at bottom left (C4). Three 3-4 points at moves 1, 3 and 5 is a pattern often seen in Shusaku’s games with Black. Dealing with White’s P17 can wait.","6":"White 6 (C14) is on the left side. Approaching Black’s bottom-left corner would be normal, but Gennan builds on the left side together with D17. It is a slightly unusual move that blocks Black’s C4 from spreading up the left side.","7":"Black 7 (E4) closes the bottom-left corner with a second stone. The corner is now almost surely Black’s. By defending before White could approach, Black also limits what White 6 can do on the left.","8":"White 8 (R5) approaches Black’s Q3. With black 3-4 points at both ends of the right side, White needs a foothold there. A long fight in the bottom right begins.","9":"Black 9 (Q15) defends the top right. Placed below White’s P17, it strengthens the corner and pushes P17 towards the narrow top edge. Black wants to keep the right side on its side.","10":"White 10 (O4) is the taisha: instead of a simple move, White caps Black’s stones diagonally from a little distance. It has so many variations that an old saying speaks of “the taisha’s hundred changes”, and it was Gennan’s speciality. How will 17-year-old Shusaku handle his opponent’s favourite weapon?","11":"With 11 (Q5) Black attaches to White’s R5 and accepts the fight. A quiet defence was possible, but Shusaku chooses the line with many variations. A reading contest follows.","12":"White 12 (Q4) wedges between Black’s stones, trying to separate Q3 from Q5 and weaken one of them.","15":"With 13 (P4) and 15 (P3), Black firmly connects the lower stones. White 14 (R4) dug into the corner, so the corner territory is now contested. Black prefers to stay connected and strong even if it gives up a little of the corner.","17":"With White 16 (P5) and Black 17 (Q6), the stones grow upwards. Black pokes its head out towards the right side, while White presses from above to keep Black in.","19":"White 18 (R3) adds a stone in the corner to make life, and Black 19 (O3) holds White back on the bottom side. Black’s plan: let White have the corner, but become strong outside along the bottom and right.","20":"White 20 (P6) caps Black’s Q6 from above, trying to shut Black low on the right side. If Black cannot get out upwards, its right-side stones will only just live, with little to gain.","21":"With 21 (Q7) and 23 (Q8), Black crawls up the right side looking for a way out, while White 22 (P7) presses alongside. Black’s long chain on the third line is low and makes little territory, but it is clearly alive.","24":"White 24 (N4) seals Black’s O3–P3 stones on the bottom from outside. Black’s stones now face two directions, the right side and the bottom, and protecting both at once is getting hard.","25":"Black 25 (N3) extends one step along the bottom. Later study says another move here, one using a ladder, would have been better, so this is called Black’s small mistake in the game. Black’s bottom stones stay low and easy to surround, which gives White a chance to strike back. Even Shusaku could go astray inside his opponent’s favourite pattern.","26":"White 26 (P8) caps Black’s right-side stones from above. Black cannot get out upwards on the right, and is in danger of being shut in on the bottom too.","28":"White 28 (M3) attaches directly to Black’s N3 — a severe move. It stops Black from spreading along the bottom and takes away living space, while making Black add more stones. Gennan punished Black’s small slip at 25 at once.","30":"After Black 29 (M2), White 30 (L2) goes round underneath as well. Black’s bottom stones can get out neither outwards nor sideways. To live they must connect to the bottom-right corner, and a capturing fight over the corner begins.","31":"Black 31 (M4) is an important move. Instead of rushing into the corner fight, Black first creates a point White must answer. It prepares for the ko that is sure to come.","33":"With 33 (R2) the capturing race in the bottom-right corner begins. White 32 (L3) made White safe on the bottom, so Black must go for White’s corner stones. If Black captures them, its bottom stones live; if not, they are in danger.","35":"White 34 (S2) and Black 35 (S1): fine moves on the first and second lines in the corner. Each side reduces the other’s liberties (empty points around a group), trying to capture first.","36":"White 36 (N2). Before starting the ko, White first plays one move around Black’s bottom stones. Even if Black wins the ko, it will gain a little less — a well-timed move.","40":"White 40 (R1) captures a stone, and at last it is a ko. In a ko the side that was just captured may not retake at once. Black must first play a threat elsewhere that White has to answer, and only then retake. The fight is decided by who has more threats.","41":"Black 41 (O5) is the first ko threat. Ignoring it would put White’s bottom-right stones in trouble, so White answers with 42 (N5), and Black retakes with 43 (R2). It costs a little, but it is necessary here.","44":"White 44 (S7) is White’s ko threat. Played beside Black’s right-side stones, it makes Black answer at 45 (S8), and White retakes at 46 (R1). Each threat settles a little more of the board.","47":"Black 47 (M5), White 48 (N6), Black 49 (R2): another threat, another retake. Around White’s bottom stones, Black is slowly adding stones on the outside.","50":"White 50 (R7) cuts into Black’s right-side stones — a big ko threat. Normally Black would answer, but Black puts the ko first. It becomes a large trade: White gains on the right side, Black gets the bottom-right corner.","51":"Black 51 (P2) captures a stone and wins the ko. The bottom-right corner becomes living Black territory, and the bottom stones are saved. In return White continues with 52 (R8) to split Black’s right-side stones in two.","53":"Black 53 (R10) is an inventive move few would find. It comes to the aid of the upper part of Black’s split right-side stones from beyond White’s stones. Had Black answered simply, White would have taken a big share of the right side.","56":"White 54 (S10), Black 55 (S11), White 56 (S9): White makes life along the right edge, while Black holds it from above and keeps the upper half of the right side.","59":"After Black 57 (M6) and White 58 (N7), Black 59 (R11) connects the right-side stones firmly. Black’s right side is now safe from top to bottom. The big trade so far looks roughly even.","60":"White 60 (S3) is a big move. It reduces Black’s corner territory while strengthening White’s stones, and it also lets White play 62 (M1) next as a move Black must answer.","63":"White 62 (M1) captures a stone and Black 63 (O1) connects: the long bottom-right fight is over for now. White has territory on the bottom; Black has a thick wall on the right. Partly because of Black 25, White is thought to have done well so far. But Shusaku’s thick wall will later work hard in the centre.","65":"White 64 (G3) extends to the left part of the bottom to make White’s bottom area even bigger. Black 65 (G5) caps it from above so White’s territory cannot grow upwards. The pattern becomes clear: White takes territory, Black takes outside strength.","66":"White 66 (C9) is on the left side. Together with White 6 (C14), it builds a White framework there too. White is gathering territory along the bottom and the left.","67":"Black 67 (L17) is on the top side. With the thick wall behind it, Black aims for a large framework — a wide area likely to become territory — from the top towards the centre. It also pincers and attacks White’s P17.","69":"White 68 (J17) goes to the left of L17 to split Black’s top framework. Black 69 (Q17) attaches to White’s P17, attacking it while strengthening the corner.","70":"White 70 (O10) goes between Black’s right-side wall and the top framework. It stops Black’s framework from joining up through the centre and tries to link with White’s stones on the right.","72":"White 72 (P13) is a superb move. Placed just above Black’s wall, it makes a White home right in the middle of Black’s framework. Even if Black chases it, White can escape lightly. Black’s hopes for the top shrink with this one move.","73":"Black 73 (P12) blocks below P13 so it cannot connect to White’s right-side stones. White 74 (Q18) then goes into the top-right corner to trim Black’s corner territory as well.","77":"After Black 75 (R18) defends the corner, White 76 (O13) and Black 77 (N11) follow. Black caps White’s centre stones, trying to gain territory while attacking. From here to around move 100, Gennan does not make a single slack move and keeps dodging Black’s attacks.","79":"White 78 (N10) connects White’s centre stones, and Black 79 (O16) firms up the top. Black did not gain much by attacking, but holds the top territory securely.","80":"With 80 (Q12) and 82 (Q13), White links stones right beside Black’s wall. Now that White has a sound shape inside Black’s framework, Black’s top-right territory will be smaller than hoped.","83":"Black 83 (H3) goes into White’s bottom territory, which had grown too large. White 84 (H2) and Black 85 (J2) follow: a close fight over whether Black can live inside or escape outside.","87":"White 86 (J3), Black 87 (H4), White 88 (K2): Black links the invading stones to G5 above and brings them out. White’s bottom territory is trimmed, and Black adds to its central thickness.","89":"Black 89 (F17) approaches White’s D17 at top left, putting a black stone on the left of the top side and stopping White from spreading along it. White 90 (M17) and 92 (N17) try to make White’s stones live between Black’s on the top.","93":"With 93 (M18) and 95 (N16), Black squeezes White’s top stones from above and below. White can only live low on the first and second lines, and even then makes very little territory.","96":"From White 96 (R14) a sharp little fight starts beside Black’s top-right corner. White splits Black’s wall from the corner to weaken Black’s stones. Black answers at 97 (Q14), and White pushes along the right edge with 98 (R12) and 100 (S12).","101":"With 101 (T12) and 103 (T13), Black descends to the first line at the right edge, narrowing White’s living space. Edge moves look small, but here they decide life and death.","104":"White 104 (Q10) hits a weak spot in Black’s right-side wall. After Black 105 (Q11) and White 106 (P11), Black goes to capture with 107.","107":"Black 107 (P10) captures a white stone. Black’s right-side wall is now solidly connected and safe. White 108 (O11) and 110 (P9) head for the centre to protect White’s stones near the wall.","112":"From White 112 (L18), White’s top stones hurry to make life. With 113 (K18), 115 (K17) and 117 (K19), Black surrounds them so they cannot get out.","118":"White 118 (M19) captures a stone and White’s top group lives. Living inside Black’s top framework cuts Black’s territory a lot. Gennan’s play so far has been excellent.","120":"Black 119 (D18) probes White’s top-left corner and White answers with 120 (C17). Black is not trying to take the corner, but to firm up the left part of the top and create a chance to add territory.","122":"Black 121 (F15) tidies Black’s shape from the top towards the centre. White 122 (C6) plays beside Black’s bottom-left corner to trim its territory bit by bit.","124":"Black 123 (G2), White 124 (J1), Black 125 (F3): the border on the left of the bottom is settled. White captures a stone there and keeps its territory safe.","126":"White 126 (J5). White is now thick on the bottom and ready to push into the centre. With territory on the bottom and left and a living group at the top right, White leads. Most of the professionals watching are said to have thought White was ahead. Black cannot catch up by ordinary play.","127":"This is the famous ear-reddening move, Black 127 (K11). This one stone in the middle of the board is said to work in four directions at once. Upwards it widens Black’s framework; downwards it stops White’s thickness from reaching the centre; to the right it supports Black’s wall; to the left it blocks White’s expansion. One move doing four jobs. From here the game, thought to favour White, slowly starts to tip towards Black.","128":"When move 127 was played, a doctor watching nearby noticed that Gennan’s ears had turned red. He knew little about go, but he is said to have read it as a sign that Gennan was shaken, and guessed that Black would win. While the professionals thought White was better, only the doctor’s eye caught the turning point. White 128 (E18) defends the top-left corner.","131":"After Black 129 (F18) firms up the left of the top, Black 131 (D10) moves into White’s left-side framework. With stone 127 holding the centre, Black can safely invade White’s territory.","136":"With 133 (E10) and 135 (D12), Black’s left-side stones slip between White’s and take shape. White 136 (J10) comes into the centre to weaken the effect of stone 127; White cannot leave the centre to Black.","140":"Black 137 (H7) caps White’s bottom stones, and White 138 (G10) and 140 (F8) try to link White’s stones on the left of the centre. In the centre the stones are tangled, and both sides have weaknesses.","143":"White 142 (E2) slips into Black’s bottom-left corner. Black 143 (D7) and 145 (D6) connect Black’s stones while eyeing both White’s left-side stones and the white invader in the corner.","147":"Black 147 (K10) extends straight down from stone 127, cutting White’s centre stones into left and right. Here the single stone of 127 begins to show its real power.","150":"White 148 (K9), Black 149 (L9), White 150 (J11): White tries to connect its centre stones, Black cuts them off from the bottom. White 150 lands right next to stone 127, pushing into Black’s centre too.","155":"From Black 151 (J9) to Black 155 (K12), Black squeezes White’s centre stones from both sides. Black 155 connects above stone 127, joining Black’s centre stones with the top. Black has taken the initiative in the centre.","160":"After White 156 (C5) and Black 157 (D5), White plays 158 (J12) and 160 (H8). White protects its centre stones while also eyeing a weakness in Black’s bottom-left stones. The game is still close; neither side can relax.","162":"Black 161 (B8) goes underneath White’s left-side stones. White 162 (D13) caps Black’s left-side stones from above to make them suffer.","167":"After strengthening the centre with 163 (G12) and 165 (J13), Black 167 (C2) is an accurate move defending the bottom-left corner. Had Black ignored it, White’s stone at 142 (E2) could have threatened the corner. Black removes the danger first and builds a hard-to-lose position.","169":"White 166 (E17) and 168 (F16) try to cut Black’s stones at the top left. Black answers with 169 (G16) and 171 (G17), connecting the top stones firmly.","175":"Black 175 (G4) captures a white stone. Black’s stones survive inside White’s bottom area, so White’s territory there is smaller than it first looked. The gap is slowly closing.","180":"White 176 (M11), Black 177 (L13), White 178 (J6), Black 179 (M10), White 180 (M13): on the right of the centre too, White puts stones into Black’s framework to reduce it. Black answers while gathering the whole centre into its own territory.","187":"From Black 183 (K13) to 187 (M14), Black presses down on the white stones in the centre and keeps them away from the top. White fights to live there, but Black’s wall is thick and the fight is hard for White.","195":"From White 190 (G14) to 194 (M9), White widens the escape routes of its centre stones. Black 195 (L10) cuts between White’s centre stones to break their shape.","199":"With Black 199 (K7) the centre fight calms down. White’s centre stones manage to live, but Black gained territory along the way and the game now leans towards Black. Next comes the endgame — fixing the borders — where one or two points decide everything.","208":"Black 203 (E19), 205 (F2), White 208 (R19): endgame moves along the edges are played from the biggest down. In the endgame it is important to play moves the opponent must answer first.","212":"With 212 (D19) and 216 (F19), White captures black stones at the top edge, and Black makes up for it elsewhere with 213 (G11) and 217 (G19). Neither side gives an inch.","225":"Black 225 (L6) captures a white stone; Black keeps trimming White’s territory in the middle of the bottom. The last big fight, a ko in the centre, is approaching.","228":"White 228 (N12) captures a stone and creates a ko in the centre. For more than 80 moves the sides will capture and recapture at M13 and N13. Each ko threat causes a small trade somewhere on the board. This long, long ko will decide the game.","236":"White 236 (T8) captures a stone in the middle of the ko. Some later analysts think that ending the ko here instead would have kept the margin smaller. In a long ko, which trades you choose decides the last point or two.","262":"With 262 (T1) and 266 (S4), White captures black stones in the bottom-right corner. Meanwhile Black has taken back elsewhere, and the ko goes on with gains and losses piling up on both sides.","275":"Black 275 (T14) captures two white stones; this trade at the top-right edge favours Black. Adding up the trades during the ko, Black is just keeping its small lead.","299":"Black 299 (J16) captures four white stones at once. White’s threat at the top is gone and Black’s top is safe. Black has come through the last big crisis of the ko.","311":"Black 311 (M13) finally fills the ko. The centre ko that lasted more than 80 moves is over. Through all the trades it caused, Black has kept the lead. Only small endgame moves remain.","321":"From Black 317 (N1) to 321 (M2), the last borders on the bottom are fixed. Both sides keep playing accurately to the end, trying to gain even one more point.","325":"The game ends after 325 moves, and the count shows Black ahead by 2 points. Black stumbled a little in Gennan’s favourite taisha early on, and White was thought to be better up to the middle game. Shusaku turned the tide with the single stone of move 127, came through the long ko and held on to a tiny lead. Gennan is said to have remarked later that Shusaku’s strength was no less than 7-dan. This game made Shusaku’s name, and he later became heir to the Honinbo house. The taisha in the opening, the ear-reddening move 127 and the long final ko — these three are the highlights of this great game."}},"evergreen":{"t":"The Evergreen Game","w":"Adolf Anderssen","b":"Jean Dufresne","ev":"A casual game (not a tournament)","d":"1852","pl":"Germany (probably Berlin)","lead":"Anderssen, of the Immortal Game, played this other masterpiece a year later. Just as Black threatens mate, White quietly moves one rook, then gives up the queen and finishes with a double check. It is called \"evergreen\" because it never fades.","c":{"0":"Germany, 1852. White is Adolf Anderssen, who had won the London tournament the year before and was thought to be the strongest player in the world. Black is his friend Jean Dufresne, who later wrote a chess handbook that many people read. It was a relaxed game, not a tournament game; it is thought to have been played in Berlin, but there is no clear record. It is another great game, born one year after “the Immortal Game”. The name is said to come from the later champion Steinitz, who praised it as “the evergreen in Anderssen’s laurel crown”. Let’s look at it together with Musowl.","1":"White pushes the pawn in front of the king two squares. It now watches d5 and f5 in the centre, and the roads for the bishop and queen are open.","2":"Black also plays e5. It faces White’s pawn in the centre and does not give way.","3":"White’s knight goes to f3. It develops a piece and attacks Black’s e5 pawn. Black has to protect this pawn.","4":"Black brings the knight to c6 to protect the e5 pawn. Defending while developing a piece: a move that wastes nothing.","5":"White’s bishop goes to c4. It aims at f7 next to Black’s king. f7 is a weak square, guarded only by the king.","6":"Black likewise brings the bishop to c5 and aims at White’s f2. Both sides have the same shape; so far it is a very calm start.","7":"b4! White offers a pawn for free: the “Evans Gambit”. It is said to have been invented by a British sea captain named Evans, and it was very popular in this era. If Black takes with the bishop, White pushes pawns to c3 and d4, gaining time while taking control of the centre.","8":"Black takes the pawn with the bishop. It is a move that accepts what is offered, but because the bishop has moved, White can now chase it and seize the initiative.","9":"With c3 White threatens the bishop on b4. At the same time, it prepares the next move, d4.","10":"The bishop drops back to a5. From a5 it looks diagonally all the way to e1 and eyes the c3 pawn from the side.","11":"d4! White pushes a pawn into the centre. It strikes at Black’s e5 pawn and tries to make the middle of the board White’s. White’s pieces can now come out one after another.","12":"Black takes the d4 pawn. Black is now two pawns up. But Black has not castled yet, and few of Black’s pieces are out.","13":"White castles. The king is tucked safely into the corner, and the rook can soon join the fight. Even two pawns down, White is far ahead in the speed of developing pieces.","14":"Black plays d3. The pawn steps forward one square and drives a wedge into White’s camp. This pawn becomes a small thorn that gets in the way of White’s pieces. Other moves are also well studied in this position, but Black chose a slightly unusual one.","15":"White’s queen goes to b3. Together with the bishop on c4 it aims at f7: a frightening setup. Since only the king guards f7, the threat of taking on f7 with the bishop with check is becoming serious.","16":"Black brings the queen to f6 to guard f7. While protecting f7 the queen also eyes White’s kingside: a hard-working square.","17":"e5! White chases away Black’s queen with a pawn. This pawn also blocks the road of Black’s knight to f6, cramping Black’s pieces.","18":"The queen escapes to g6. From here it aims down the g-file at g2 in front of White’s king, and it also keeps an eye on e4.","19":"The rook goes to e1 and firmly protects the e5 pawn. White’s rook has now joined the fight too.","20":"Black develops the knight to e7. Next Black would like to castle and make the king safe. The knight went to e7 instead of f6 because the e5 pawn controls f6.","21":"The bishop goes to a3! There is a straight diagonal road from a3 toward f8. This bishop aims at the knight on e7 and also looks at f8 behind it. If the knight moves, the road to f8 opens, so it has become hard for Black to castle on the kingside. A very important move that keeps Black’s king trapped in the middle.","22":"With b5 Black offers a pawn and threatens the bishop on c4. It is a counterattack to pull White’s queen away from f7 and shake things up.","23":"White takes the pawn with the queen. The queen protects the bishop on c4 while still aiming near Black’s king.","24":"Black’s rook comes out to b8 and threatens the queen on b5. The rook has started working on the open b-file.","25":"The queen goes to a4. It attacks the bishop on a5, and along the diagonal from a4 to e8 it pins the c6 knight to Black’s king. If this knight moves, the king would be in check, so it cannot move.","26":"The bishop escapes from a5 to b6. It is back on its old diagonal, aiming toward f2.","27":"White develops the knight to d2. The last piece left at home comes out, and next it plans to jump to e4 and approach Black’s king.","28":"Black develops the bishop to b7 to protect the pinned knight on c6. The long diagonal from b7 is blocked by the c6 knight for now, but if the knight moves, the bishop will be able to aim toward f3 and g2.","29":"The knight goes to e4! From the middle of the board it threatens to jump to f6 or d6 with check. Black’s king is still in the centre and in danger.","30":"Black’s queen goes to f5. It aims at both the e4 knight and the f3 knight. The idea is to make the knights step aside and then go after White’s king.","31":"The bishop takes the pawn on d3. The pawn that was Black’s thorn is gone. What’s more, this bishop is looking at the f5 queen from behind the e4 knight: if the knight moves, the bishop will take the queen.","32":"The queen escapes to h5. From here it aims through g4 at the knight on f3. The plan is to take f3 with the queen next and use the g-file too, to mate White’s king.","33":"The knight jumps to f6 with check! It also attacks the queen on h5: a fork. So as not to lose the queen, Black decides to take this knight.","34":"Black takes the knight with the g7 pawn. The g-file is open now, so Black can aim at White’s king with the rook on g8.","35":"White recaptures on f6 with the e5 pawn. This pawn threatens the knight on e7, and since the e-file is open, the rook on e1 now looks through the e7 knight at Black’s king. The f6 pawn also controls the squares g7 and e7.","36":"Black’s rook goes to g8. On the open g-file it aims at g2 in front of White’s king. If the queen takes f3 next, g2 will be attacked by both the queen and the rook, and Black looks set to mate White.","37":"The rook goes to d1! Black has a mating threat, yet White quietly moves a rook. Between d1 and d7 stands only the bishop on d3. If the bishop moves, the rook will look straight at the square next to Black’s king. It is a deeply calculated move that aims to finish first with a series of checks, and it is the most famous move of this game. However, later studies say there were other moves for both White and Black, and it is still being examined today.","38":"Black’s queen takes the knight on f3. If it takes g2 next, it is mate together with the rook. White now has to win by giving check after check.","39":"The rook takes the knight on e7 with check! Even giving up a piece, White keeps chasing Black’s king.","40":"Black recaptures with the c6 knight. But because the knight left c6, the diagonal from the a4 queen to d7 is now open. It is said that even if Black moved the king instead of taking with the knight, White’s attack would go on.","41":"White sacrifices the queen on d7 with check! Through the diagonal that has just opened, the queen jumps right in front of Black’s king. This is the decisive blow of the Evergreen.","42":"Black takes the queen with the king. Running to f8 would allow the queen to take the e7 knight with mate, so taking was the only choice.","43":"The bishop moves to f5: double check! The bishop checks the king on d7 from f5, and the rook on d1, uncovered by the bishop, also checks along the d-file. When two pieces give check at once, you cannot defend by blocking or capturing; the king must run.","44":"The king runs to e8. If it ran to c6 instead, the bishop would come to d7 and it would be mate.","45":"The bishop comes to d7 with check. This time it is right next to the king on e8. This bishop is protected by the rook on d1, so the king cannot take it.","46":"The king goes to f8. Running to d8 would also lose: the a3 bishop would take the e7 knight with mate.","47":"The a3 bishop takes the knight on e7: checkmate! The d7 bishop controls e8, the f6 pawn controls e7 and g7, and Black’s own pieces block f7 and g8. The f6 pawn, the bishop that had not moved since it came to a3, and the rook on d1: every piece worked perfectly at the end. Black was aiming at g2 with the queen and rook, but was one move too late. Reading the opponent’s threat and then finishing first with a chain of checks: this game is a model of how to attack."}},"lasker1912":{"t":"The King Hunt","w":"Edward Lasker","b":"George Alan Thomas","ev":"A quick casual game at the City of London Chess Club","d":"1912","pl":"United Kingdom (London)","lead":"A fast, casual game at a London club. White gives up the queen and chases Black's king all the way across the board, from g8 to g1. The opening moves are written in slightly different orders in different records, but the position after move 10 is the same.","c":{"0":"October 1912, the City of London Chess Club. White is Edward Lasker, a young engineer just arrived from Germany; Black is George Alan Thomas, later British champion. It was a fast, casual game. It is famous as a \"king hunt\": Black's king is chased from g8 all the way to g1 at the other end of the board. Lasker later moved to America and became well known for his chess books.","1":"White starts with d4, the queen's pawn. It covers e5 and c5 and opens the way for the bishop on c1.","2":"Black plays e6, building a solid position and preparing f5 or d5.","3":"Knight to f3. It covers e5 and helps guard the king's side: the most natural square.","4":"Black plays f5, controlling e4, like the Dutch Defence. But moving a pawn in front of the king makes Black's king a little weaker.","5":"The other knight comes to c3, getting ready for e4 to hit the f5 pawn.","6":"Black brings a knight to f6. It controls e4 even more and prepares to castle.","7":"Bishop to g5, aiming at the knight on f6. That knight is an important defender of Black's king, so White wants to remove it early.","8":"Black brings the bishop to e7 to guard the knight, and gets closer to castling.","9":"White trades the bishop for the knight. The knight that guarded Black's king is gone.","10":"Black takes back with the bishop. Now this bishop on f6 takes over the job of guarding the king's side.","11":"e4! White hits the f5 pawn and gains space in the centre. Lines open, and White's pieces can move more freely.","12":"Black takes the pawn on e4. If not, White would push e5 and drive Black's pieces back.","13":"The knight takes back and sits in the centre, eyeing the bishop on f6 and the squares g5 and d6.","14":"Black plays b6, preparing to bring the bishop to b7 to aim down the long diagonal at the knight on e4.","15":"Knight to e5, a strong central square that also eyes g4 and f7. Both white knights are now close to Black's king's side.","16":"Black castles. The king is on g8. But with the f-pawn gone, there are many gaps around the king.","17":"Bishop to d3. For now its own knight on e4 is in the way, but behind it lies the pawn on h7. The moment the knight on e4 moves, this bishop will aim straight at h7.","18":"Black's bishop goes to b7 and aims down the long diagonal at the knight on e4, making White's pieces less free.","19":"Queen to h5, aiming at the pawn on h7, which only Black's king defends. White's queen, bishop and knights all point at Black's king.","20":"Black puts the queen on e7. It guards the bishop on f6 and gets ready to go to c5, but Black misses White's idea. Moves like h6 or taking the knight on e5 were needed. This is the turning point.","21":"The queen is given up on h7, with check! White hands over the strongest piece for nothing. Lasker saw the whole path of chasing the king to mate.","22":"Black must take with the king. The king is pulled out to h7.","23":"The knight takes the bishop on f6 with check. At the same time e4 is empty, so the bishop on d3 also checks the king on h7: a double check. It cannot be blocked, so the king must run.","24":"The king goes to h6. On h8, the other knight would jump to g6 and mate. The king is pushed further and further forward.","25":"The other knight jumps from e5 to g4 with check. It attacks the king on h6 and drives it further forward.","26":"The king goes to g5, leaving its own camp and walking toward White's side.","27":"Check with the h-pawn! Even the pawns join the hunt. The king has to keep moving forward.","28":"The king goes to f4. It has crossed the middle of the board and is entering White's camp.","29":"Check again with the g-pawn. Two pawns now block the way back for the king.","30":"The king goes to f3, right in the middle of White's pieces.","31":"The bishop drops back from d3 to e2 with check. The king is pushed even deeper.","32":"The king goes to g2. White's own king on e1 is not far away.","33":"Rook to h2, check. At last the rook joins in.","34":"The king finally reaches g1. It started on g8 and has walked to the first row at the other end of the board.","35":"White's king steps from e1 to d2. This opens the way for the rook on a1, which now checks the king on g1 along the first row: checkmate! Castling queenside would also have mated, but Lasker moved the king. In a quick game, after giving up the queen, White chased the king for fourteen moves into the deepest corner of White's own camp. A very rare game."}},"toketsu":{"t":"The Blood-vomiting Game","w":"Akaboshi Intetsu","b":"Honinbo Jowa","ev":"Played at the residence of Lord Matsudaira of Suo (over four days)","d":"1835","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"A game born of the struggle for Meijin Godokoro, the highest post in Japanese go. Inoue Gennan Inseki sent his pupil Akaboshi Intetsu to face the Meijin, Jowa. After four days of hard fighting Intetsu lost, and he is said to have coughed up blood at the board.","c":{"0":"Edo, 1835. This game was played at a go gathering in the residence of Lord Matsudaira of Suo, a senior minister. Black is Akaboshi Intetsu (7-dan) of the Inoue house; White is Honinbo Jowa, holder of Meijin Godokoro, the highest post in go, which he had won in a struggle with Inoue Gennan Inseki. Unhappy with that, Gennan sent his best pupil, Intetsu, planning to argue that a Meijin who lost to a 7-dan did not deserve the title. The game lasted four days and left one of the most famous — and saddest — stories in go history.","1":"Black 1 on the 3-4 point at bottom left (C4). Intetsu starts calmly, showing he will play his own game even against the Meijin.","2":"White 2 (Q3), Black 3 (R16), White 4 (E16): one stone goes into each corner. White 4 is on the high 4-5 point, valuing outward spread over corner territory.","5":"Black 5 (P17) encloses the top-right corner with a second stone. Black makes the top right solid to prepare for the fights ahead.","6":"White 6 (E3) already approaches Black’s bottom-left corner. Jowa is aggressive from the start and gives Black no time to settle quietly.","8":"Black 7 (R5) approaches White’s Q3, and White 8 (R9) pincers it from above. White wants to make Black’s stone weak on the right side and turn it into a target.","9":"Black 9 (R11) pincers White 8 back from above. With Black’s top-right corner solid, Black can turn the tables and attack White on the right side.","10":"White 10 (P4) defends the bottom-right corner, leaving the right-side fight for later and making its own corner solid first.","11":"Black 11 (J3) goes to the middle of the bottom side, between White’s E3 and P4, so that White cannot take a big area there.","12":"White 12 (D6) presses down on Black’s bottom-left corner from above. Together with E3, it tries to wrap up Black’s C4. The biggest fight of the game begins here.","13":"Black 13 (E4) attaches right between White’s stones and splits them before Black can be surrounded. Black accepts the fight head-on.","16":"White 14 (D4), Black 15 (D5), White 16 (D3): White digs under Black’s corner while Black grows upwards. With stones cut on both sides, each has weak groups — a difficult fight.","19":"Black 17 (C5), White 18 (E5), Black 19 (F4): Black holds White’s E5 from below and keeps White’s stones split. White’s bottom-left stones must either live in the corner or escape outward.","20":"White 20 (C3) slips under Black’s corner, eating its territory while trying to live there. Black 21 (B3) and White 22 (B2) continue the close exchange inside the corner.","22":"White 22 (B2) drops to the second line in the corner to shape White’s life there. Black’s corner is now smaller, but Black’s outside stones are stronger.","23":"Black 23 (C7) goes up the left side and holds White’s upper stones (like D6) from below. White 24 (G2) extends along the bottom, giving the corner stones a way to connect and live.","26":"Black 25 (H4) caps White’s bottom stones, and White 26 (E6) strengthens the upper white stones. The lower white group wants territory, the upper one an exit to the centre — and Black is eyeing both.","27":"Black 27 (B4) blocks above White’s corner stones, narrowing their living space to prepare later attacks.","28":"White 28 (C9) reaches far along the left side, trying to give the upper white stones a base to live on. It is often called an over-extension — a fierce move very typical of Jowa. Black now sets out to punish it.","31":"Black 29 (D8), White 30 (D9), Black 31 (E8): Black cuts in between White’s C9 and the upper white stones, splitting White in two. White’s C9–D9 on the left and D6–E6 in the centre are now separated.","33":"Black 33 (C2). This is said to be a new line that the Inoue house had studied in secret. It squeezes White’s corner stones and at the same time attacks White’s floating stones in the centre — two aims at once. A “secret move” prepared to defeat the Meijin, and from here Black’s advantage becomes clear.","36":"White 34 (D2), Black 35 (B1), White 36 (F3): White hurries to connect its bottom stones and make life. Meanwhile White’s centre stones get no help and become Black’s target.","38":"White 38 (A2) is a first-line move that secures eye space for White’s corner stones. Once the lower white group is settled, White can focus on the upper fight.","40":"From Black 37 (G5) to White 40 (G7), White’s centre stones run upwards while Black chases closely alongside, strengthening its own stones as it goes.","42":"Black 41 (H7), White 42 (G8): White runs upwards into the centre and Black follows from outside. The further White goes up, the firmer Black’s bottom and left become.","43":"Black 43 (E9). White’s C9–D9 on the left are surrounded and in trouble, and White’s centre stones are not yet alive either. Up to here Intetsu has played splendidly and is thought to have taken a clear lead. Against the Meijin, the Inoue plan seemed to be working.","45":"White 44 (H8) runs the centre stones upwards, and Black 45 (D11) extends up the left side, blocking above C9–D9. White’s left-side stones are losing their way out both upwards and towards the centre.","48":"Black 47 (H5), White 48 (F5): White tries to link its centre and bottom stones, while Black tries to pry them apart. For White, every connection counts.","50":"Black 49 (C12) makes the left side even stronger, and White 50 (C16) turns to the top-left corner. White leaves the hard bottom-left fight for now to gain elsewhere.","53":"Black 51 (M3) reinforces the bottom, and White 52 (Q6) presses Black’s R5 on the right. Black 53 (G17) goes into the top side to stop White’s top left from growing.","57":"After White 54 (R6) firms up the right side, Black 55 (D17), White 56 (C17), Black 57 (E17): Black plays right above White’s top-left corner, trimming it while spreading along the top.","58":"White 58 (C14) spreads from the top-left corner along the side towards Black’s left-side stones. White’s top left and Black’s left side now meet head-on.","60":"When Black 59 (J8) caps White’s centre stones, White 60 (G4) captures two black stones. White’s bottom-left stones finally get some breathing room — a struggling White takes what it can.","63":"Black 61 (O3) adds a stone on the right of the bottom, White 62 (J17) goes to the top, and Black 63 (F16) holds White’s top left from outside. Sparks of fighting spread all over the board.","66":"White 64 (M17), Black 65 (B13), White 66 (B14): Black slips under White’s stones at the left edge while White tries to settle the top-left group. White’s C9–D9 are still surrounded.","68":"White 68 (B11). The next few white moves, together with move 80, became famous as Jowa’s brilliant moves — so remarkable that people said ghosts had shown them to him. B11 looks for a path linking White’s surrounded C9–D9 on the left with the top-left group. From here, the struggling White hits back.","70":"Black 69 (D10) tries to stop the connection, but White 70 (E12) ignores the left side and takes a key point in the centre first. Hidden resources remain for White on the left, so there is no need to answer at once. Taking back the initiative while letting the attack slide — a Meijin’s move.","72":"After Black 71 (F10) presses White’s centre, White 72 (R13) turns to the right side. White pauses the centre fight to stop Black’s top-right framework from growing.","76":"Black 73 (P11), White 74 (R15), Black 75 (Q16), White 76 (O14): White slips beside Black’s top-right corner and places stones linking the right side with the centre — groundwork for splitting Black’s stones.","78":"Black 77 (P9), White 78 (Q9): White blocks the link between Black’s right-side stones (R5, P9) and the black stones above. Will Black connect, or be cut apart? A tense moment.","80":"White 80 (Q10) makes an “empty triangle”, a clump of three stones usually considered bad shape. Yet it became the base for splitting Black’s right-side stones in two and attacking them. Valuing function over looks, it is often shown as the classic bad-looking but excellent move — the last of the famous three. From here White’s splitting attack begins in earnest, and the game slowly starts to lean towards White.","83":"After Black 81 (S6) and White 82 (S7), Black 83 (R3) goes into White’s bottom-right corner. Under pressure on the right, Black seeks both life and territory by invading White’s corner.","86":"Black 85 (S2), White 86 (R4): Black reduces White’s liberties from the first line, White holds Black from inside the corner. Both are counting how many moves each group has before capture.","88":"From White 84 (R2) to White 88 (Q2), a close capturing race goes on along the first and second lines of the corner. Who captures first? One move can make the difference.","91":"Black 91 (Q4) captures a white stone and gets one step ahead in the corner race. But White is preparing to wrap Black’s stones from outside.","95":"White 92 (N4), Black 93 (R7), White 94 (Q7), Black 95 (O4): White goes round Black’s outside from the bottom, while Black tries to connect on both the right and the bottom. The bigger Black’s group grows, the harder it is to save.","98":"White 98 (M4) wraps Black’s bottom stones from outside. Black has no quick safe shape either in the bottom-right corner or towards the centre.","101":"From White 96 (N3) to Black 101 (O5), black and white stones grow side by side from the bottom towards the centre — a pushing match where each holds the other from one side.","104":"After Black 103 (P2), White 104 (R4) captures a black stone. In the bottom-right corner race, White has taken a step back.","106":"Black 105 (N2), White 106 (S5) captures a black stone. White holds the bottom-right corner; Black’s stones cannot live there and must run outward.","107":"Black 107 (Q11) protects Black’s upper right-side stones: with the lower group in trouble, Black at least makes the upper one safe. White 108 (M2) and Black 109 (O1) continue a small capturing fight at the bottom edge.","113":"After White 110 (J4), Black 111 (K2) and White 112 (H3), Black 113 (K6): Black’s bottom stones poke out towards the centre looking for life. They must secure an exit before White closes the net.","116":"White 114 (L5), Black 115 (L3), White 116 (L6): White puts a lid over Black’s stones and narrows the exit to the centre. Black cuts White’s bottom stones while trying to get out.","120":"Black 117 (K7), White 118 (L7), Black 119 (J9), White 120 (N9): Black leaps out into the centre and White runs ahead to block from the right. It is now a race over whose stones weaken first.","125":"With White 122 (J2) and Black 123 (K3), Black’s bottom stones just manage to connect. Black 125 (N11) tries to link the upper right-side group with the centre stones.","128":"White 126 (O7), Black 127 (N8), White 128 (L11): White goes round above Black’s centre stones too, pressing them from above and below. The splitting plan prepared since move 80 is taking shape.","132":"From Black 129 (M10) to White 132 (K10), the two sides cut each other in the centre. Black tries to break White’s ring; White closes Black’s exits one by one.","137":"From Black 133 (L8) to White 136 (P10), the centre gets even more tangled. Black 137 (N13) jumps far upwards, trying to link the centre stones with Black’s top.","138":"White 138 (K8) captures a stone and a ko begins in the centre. For about 30 moves the sides take and retake at K8 and L8. This ko will decide the fate of Black’s centre stones.","143":"From Black 139 (K9) to Black 143 (J6), threats and retakes continue. Black 143 captures a white stone and eases the pressure on Black’s bottom stones.","148":"From White 144 (K5) to White 148 (K8), ko threats and retakes continue at the bottom and centre. With each threat, White gains a little around Black’s bottom stones.","152":"White 152 (E11) is a ko threat, hitting a weak point in Black’s left-side stones. Throughout the ko, White keeps finding moves all over the board that Black has to answer.","158":"From Black 155 (H2) to White 158 (C6), ko threats in the bottom and bottom left cause small trades. White 158 threatens Black’s bottom-left corner, and Black defends with 159 (B6).","162":"Black 161 (G1), White 162 (F1): captures happen at the bottom edge too. During the ko, small fights spread to every corner of the board.","167":"After White 166 (K8) retakes again, Black 167 (M1) does not continue the ko but plays elsewhere on the bottom. White thus wins the centre ko and can connect its centre stones. Black gains something on the bottom instead, but its centre stones are now in trouble.","170":"After White 168 (K11) and Black 169 (H10), White 170 (P15) heads for the top right. White settles the centre for now and aims at the big black group there, which is not yet fully alive.","175":"From Black 171 (L13) to Black 175 (K14), Black extends towards the top to link the top-right group with the centre stones. White 174 (O17) narrows Black’s living space near the top-right corner.","178":"White 178 (J14) jumps into the middle of the top to split Black’s stones left and right. The splitting attack prepared since move 80 now begins in earnest. Black must defend both sides at once — a very hard position.","182":"From Black 179 (K15) to White 182 (G14), White presses Black from outside on the left of the top as well. Black’s top-left stones also have to look for living space.","190":"From Black 183 (F14) to White 190 (J13), the two sides cut each other fiercely on the left of the top. White keeps placing stones between Black’s, breaking up Black’s shape.","193":"Black 193 (J11) captures a white stone; Black fights hard to connect and find life. But Black’s stones on the right of the top are still uneasy.","199":"From White 194 (S14) to Black 199 (A16), White holds Black’s top-right corner from outside while Black works at the top-left edge too. Which big group weakens first? A key moment before the end.","201":"Black 201 (R14) captures a stone and a new ko begins on the right side at R14 and R15. Whether Black’s top-right group lives depends on this ko.","206":"From White 202 (E14) to White 206 (M13), White plays ko threats while slowly tightening the grip on Black’s centre stones. Even during the ko, White’s overall net is nearly closed.","213":"From Black 207 (L17) to Black 213 (K18), Black fights to connect on the top. White reaches down to the first and second lines to rob Black of eyes — the empty points a group needs to live.","222":"White 222 (L14) captures a stone. Black’s top stones remain split in two, and saving both is now very hard. The Meijin’s splitting attack is finally paying off.","225":"Black 225 (R15) resolves the right-side ko and the top-right group manages to live. But meanwhile White has taken the middle of the top, and overall White is still ahead.","232":"Black 229 (P14) and White 232 (Q14) start another small ko in the top right. Black keeps looking for chances to the end, but White answers carefully.","239":"With 237 (F12) and 239 (E13), Black captures back on the top left. Even so, it does not make up for what was lost in the middle of the top, and the overall gap does not shrink.","244":"Black 241 (P18), White 242 (P19), Black 243 (P14), White 244 (N12): small moves continue to the end, but White plays the important centre points one by one. The game is almost decided.","246":"After White 246 (L8), Intetsu resigned. Early on he took a big lead with the Inoue house’s secret line, but Jowa hit back with his brilliant moves from 68 and the ugly-but-excellent move 80, kept splitting and attacking Black’s stones and finally turned the game around. As the stones were being cleared, Intetsu is said to have coughed up blood onto the board. He had probably been ill for some time, and he died not long afterwards. For Jowa, who kept the Meijin title, it is called the greatest win of his life, and Gennan’s plan failed. It is still told today as the game young Intetsu played with his life."}},"deepblue1997":{"t":"Deep Blue Wins","w":"Deep Blue (IBM computer)","b":"Garry Kasparov","ev":"Game 6 of the rematch with IBM's Deep Blue (a six-game match)","d":"1997","pl":"United States (New York)","lead":"World champion Kasparov against Deep Blue, a chess computer built by IBM, in a six-game match. With the score level after five games, the last game was over in only 19 moves. It was the first time a computer beat the world champion in a full match of several games.","c":{"0":"New York, 11 May 1997. World champion Garry Kasparov against Deep Blue, a chess computer built by IBM, in a six-game match. Kasparov had won their match the year before, but in this rematch against the stronger Deep Blue the score was 2½–2½ after five games. In the last game, Deep Blue had White. The whole world watched to see whether a human or a computer was stronger.","1":"White pushes the king's pawn two squares, taking the centre and opening paths for the pieces.","2":"Kasparov plays c6, getting ready for d5 to hit e4: the Caro-Kann Defence. It is solid, and players often chose it against computers.","3":"White brings the other pawn to the centre too. Two pawns stand side by side.","4":"Black plays d5 and hits e4. The fight for the centre pawns begins.","5":"The knight goes to c3 and guards e4. A natural move: develop and defend.","6":"Black takes the pawn on e4, trading centre pawns to make White's centre smaller.","7":"The knight takes back. White's knight now stands in the middle of the board.","8":"Black puts a knight on d7. Then, when the other knight goes to f6, Black can take back with this knight if White trades knights.","9":"The knight jumps to g5, a sharp plan aiming at f7 and e6. White attacks early, before Black's king side is settled.","10":"Black brings the knight to f6. Now more pieces guard the king.","11":"Bishop to d3. It aims diagonally at h7 and points toward Black's king side.","12":"Black plays e6 to strengthen the defence and open the way for the bishop on f8. But the e6 pawn also becomes a target for White's knight.","13":"The other knight goes from g1 to f3. White is ready to castle.","14":"Black plays h6 to chase the knight on g5. But in this position a knight sacrifice on e6 was already known for White. People found it strange that a master like Kasparov chose this, and some think he mixed up the move order by accident. This is the turning point.","15":"The knight is given up on e6! In return, White breaks the pawns around Black's king and stops Black from castling. Deep Blue is said to have played this move from the opening moves it had stored in advance.","16":"Black doesn't take at once but puts the queen on e7. On the e-file it lines up on the knight on e6 and White's king behind it. If the knight moves, the king is in check, so the knight cannot escape.","17":"White castles and moves the king away. Now the knight on e6 is no longer pinned. White does not mind if the knight is taken.","18":"Black takes the knight and is ahead in material. But the pawns around Black's king are broken, and the king is poorly protected.","19":"Bishop to g6, check. With the f7 pawn gone, the diagonal reaches the king on e8.","20":"The king goes to d8. Black can no longer castle, and the king is stuck near the middle. Black's pieces get in each other's way around the king.","21":"The other bishop goes to f4, eyeing c7 and b8 and squeezing the space around Black's king even more.","22":"Black plays b5, making room on the queen's side to bring the bishop to b7 and give the king a way out.","23":"a4 hits the pawn on b5. White wants to open lines on the queen's side so the rook on a1 can join the attack.","24":"Black brings the bishop to b7, aiming down the long diagonal at g2. Black's pieces are finally starting to move.","25":"Rook to e1. On the e-file it eyes the e6 pawn and the queen on e7 behind it. All of White's pieces have joined the attack.","26":"Black's knight goes to d5, a strong central square, attacking the bishop on f4 and protecting e6.","27":"The bishop drops back to g3. It keeps aiming at c7 and b8, keeping the pressure on Black's king.","28":"Black's king goes to c8, a little safer, and lets the rook on d8 move.","29":"White takes the pawn on b5. The a-file opens, and the rook on a1 now eyes the pawn on a7.","30":"Black takes back with a pawn. But fewer and fewer pawns are left around Black's king.","31":"Queen to d3, eyeing the b5 pawn and the square f5. White's pieces slowly surround Black's king.","32":"Black brings the bishop back to c6 to guard the b5 pawn.","33":"Bishop to f5! It can be taken for free. But if a pawn takes it, the e-file pawn is gone, and the rook on e1 reaches the queen on e7 directly.","34":"Black takes with a pawn. Now nothing stands on the e-file between e1 and e7.","35":"The rook takes the queen on e7. White has given a knight, a bishop and a rook, but has won the strongest piece, the queen.","36":"Black takes back the rook with the bishop. Material is about even, but Black's king has almost no shelter.","37":"c4! The pawn hits both b5 and d5, and Black's defenders start to fall apart. White's queen is ready to join the attack at any moment. Kasparov resigned here, probably feeling his king was too open to stop the attack. Only 19 moves. It was the first time a computer beat the world champion in a full match of several games: a historic moment. After this, using computers to study and train became normal in chess."}},"seigen1933":{"t":"The 3-3, Star Point and Tengen Game","w":"Go Seigen","b":"Honinbo Shusai","ev":"Commemorative game with the Meijin (over three months)","d":"1933–1934","pl":"Japan (Tokyo)","lead":"Nineteen-year-old Go Seigen (5-dan) played the Meijin, Honinbo Shusai. With Black he opened on the 3-3 point, then a star point, then the very centre (tengen) — breaking every rule of the day and astonishing the go world. With many adjournments the game lasted over three months, and White won by 2 points.","c":{"0":"Tokyo, autumn 1933. Black is 19-year-old Go Seigen (5-dan); White is the 59-year-old Meijin, Honinbo Shusai. Go Seigen was born in Fujian, China, and came to Japan at 14 as a child prodigy. That year he and his friend Kitani Minoru were spreading a new way of playing called “Shin Fuseki” (New Opening), which valued power in the centre more than territory in the corners, and it had become the talk of the go world. Shusai was the last Meijin to hold the Honinbo name by inheritance, and stood at the very top of go. Everyone wanted to see how the new generation would challenge the old master. The game was adjourned many times (stopped and continued on a later day) and lasted more than three months. There was no komi in those days, so only the territory on the board decides the result.","1":"Black 1 is the 3-3 point in the top right (R17), where the third lines from both edges cross. One stone there almost surely secures the corner territory. In return it has little power towards the outside, and at the time it was thought “not a move to play first”. Against the Meijin, Go Seigen began with a 3-3 right away.","2":"White 2 is the 3-4 point (komoku) in the top left (C16), where the third and fourth lines cross. It is the most basic opening move, played since the Edo period. The Meijin showed no surprise and chose his usual move.","3":"Black 3 is the star point in the bottom left (D4), where the two fourth lines cross. Unlike the 3-3, it values spreading outwards more than territory. Diagonally opposite the 3-3, Black placed a stone of a quite different character.","4":"White 4 takes the bottom-right corner with another 3-4 point (Q3). White plans to take the corners one by one and get ahead in territory. Whatever new things Black tries, the Meijin’s plan does not waver.","5":"Black 5 goes to the very centre of the board: tengen (K10). Tengen is equally close to every side, so it can lend strength to a fight in any direction. But it hardly becomes territory, so almost no one played it in the opening. 3-3, star point, tengen: with three stones Black displayed three ideas side by side — corner territory, outward spread, and central power. This daring opening caused a sensation, and it is said that some people even called it disrespectful to the Meijin.","6":"White 6 (Q16) is a “shoulder hit”, leaning diagonally on Black’s 3-3 stone in the top right. White wants to shut Black in low in the corner and build a thick wall outside. This is the standard way to meet a 3-3, and the Meijin went straight for the starting point of Black’s new opening.","8":"Black 7 (R16) crawls and White 8 (Q15) extends. Black widens its living space in the corner while White stretches its wall towards the right side. Black does not push outwards here because it has clearly given the 3-3 stone the job of “corner territory”.","9":"Black 9 (P18) jumps along the second line on the top side, widening the corner territory and making a firm living shape. Now the top right is clearly divided: the corner is Black’s territory, the outside is White’s thickness.","10":"White 10 (R6) sits where it works with both the wall in the top right (Q16, Q15) above and the stone in the bottom right (Q3) below. It is a calm, large move aiming to make the whole right side White’s domain.","11":"Black 11 (K4) is the middle of the bottom side, on the fourth line right below tengen. Lined up vertically with K10, it aims at a large framework from the bottom side up into the centre. All of Black’s stones stand high, showing clearly that Black values influence over territory.","12":"White 12 (M3) approaches from the bottom-right corner, landing on the third line just right of Black’s K4. It is a move to get a foothold early so that Black cannot enclose the bottom side. Left alone, Black might play around here next and turn the bottom side into a big territory.","13":"Black 13 (D10) takes the middle of the left side. With D4 and D10 on the left and K4 and K10 in the centre, Black’s stones now spread like a net over the lower-left half of the board. Not answering White 12 at once and taking a big point first is very much the spirit of the New Opening.","14":"White 14 (E16) adds a second stone in the top-left corner, enclosing it (a shimari). The Meijin is not distracted by Black’s new style and keeps making corners surely his own. By now the plans are sharply split: White has two corners and the right side, Black two corners and the centre.","15":"Black 15 (M5) caps White’s M3 from above. Black wants to press White down low and build a wall outside. The idea: even if White gets some territory on the bottom side, Black turns that into power in the centre.","18":"White 16 (O3) widens the foothold towards the White stone at Q3, and with Black 17 (L3) and White 18 (M4) the stones clash on the bottom side. White makes a living shape while securing the third line; Black presses from above. Both sides keep to their own plans.","20":"White 20 (C12) slides from the top-left enclosure down the left side along the third line. It approaches Black’s D10 and stops the whole left side from becoming Black’s. The Meijin’s method was to take territory bit by bit from the edges before Black’s framework could grow.","21":"Black 21 (G10) sits exactly between D10 on the left side and K10 at tengen. Black’s stones now stand in a row at fourth-line height, forming a big wall from the left side into the centre. Black ignores White’s territory and keeps widening its central influence.","22":"White 22 (F3) approaches Black’s star point D4 in the bottom left from the bottom side (a kakari). White already has stones in the bottom right and on the bottom side, so making territory here too would give White the whole bottom edge. It shaves the lowest rim of Black’s big framework.","25":"Black 23 (F5) caps White’s F3; when White 24 (J3) extends right along the bottom, Black 25 (G4) presses from above again. White on the third line, Black on the fourth and fifth: the pattern of this game — White takes territory, Black takes walls — repeats here too.","26":"White 26 (C3) is the 3-3 point inside Black’s star point D4 in the bottom left. Slipping in right under the star stone, White goes for the corner territory. A star point has strong outward power, but its weakness is that it cannot keep the corner when the 3-3 is invaded.","28":"Black 27 (C4) blocks from outside and White 28 (D3) makes a living shape in the corner. Black gives the corner territory to White and in return gains a thick wall facing the centre: C4, D4, F5, G4. Looking over the opening, White has territory in the top-left, bottom-right and bottom-left corners and on the bottom side, while Black has the top-right corner and central influence. How much of that central influence becomes territory will decide the game from here on.","29":"Black 29 (O16) is played on the top side, to the left of White’s wall in the top right (Q16, Q15). It keeps the wall from working along the top side and widens Black’s top-right corner along the top edge. It stands at a sensible distance, not too close to White’s thickness.","30":"White 30 (Q12) extends down the right side from the top-right wall and makes a base (a foothold for life). Now the top-right White stones and R6 can link up more easily, and the right side starts to look like White territory.","31":"Black 31 (Q10) lands on the right-side star point, right between White’s Q12 and R6. It stops White from turning the whole right side into territory, and it also works together with Black’s central influence. From here the right side becomes a battlefield where Black and White stones mix.","33":"White 32 (O15) attaches under Black’s O16, shaking the root of Black’s top-side stone. Black 33 (N15) blocks from the left, closing White’s road to the left of the top side and the centre. By keeping White’s stones from getting out, Black wants the top side as its sphere of influence.","35":"When White 34 (N14) extends downwards, Black 35 (O14) cuts between White’s O15 and N14. Cut apart, White’s stones split into two, and each becomes weak. Black makes White fight inside Black’s own sphere, hoping to profit while attacking.","37":"After White 36 (O13), Black 37 (P15) captures White’s O15 — the first prisoner of the game. White 38 (P14) is answered by Black 39 (O15), connecting, and Black’s top-right stones are now firmly joined to the top side. White lost a stone but has poked out into the centre with O13, N13 and N14.","40":"White 40 (O10) jumps far into the centre from the O13 stone, landing between Black’s Q10 on the right side and tengen. White now has a foothold inside Black’s central framework. For Black, there is also the pleasure of attacking these White stones inside its own sphere.","41":"Black 41 (P7) is played low on the right side, pressing White’s R6 from above and working with Q10. Now White’s right-side stones, Q12 above and R6 below, are squeezed by Black and hard to connect. Black is trying to shrink White’s territory on the right side as well.","43":"After White 42 (N13) connects and settles the central White stones, Black 43 (L15) extends left along the top side. Black’s top-side wall grows longer, and the area from the top side into the centre becomes Black’s sphere. Black does not rush the fight and first enlarges its own domain.","44":"White 44 (H17) extends from the top-left enclosure along the top side, stopping Black’s top-side influence before it can grow further. Here too the Meijin places a stone on the side, where territory is easy to make, before Black’s framework spreads. Another rim of Black’s big framework has been taken by White.","45":"Black 45 (R7) attaches right on top of White’s R6 on the right side. This starts the first big fight of the game. Black aims to cut R6 off from the White stones above (Q12) and split White’s right side in two. White does not back down either, and from here a tricky shape of mutually cut stones continues from the right side into the bottom right.","48":"White 46 (S7), Black 47 (S6), White 48 (S8). White places stones on the second line below and above Black’s R7 to gain strength around it. Black’s S6 goes round to the lower right of White’s R6, taking away the space R6 needed to live easily along the edge. Each side is poking at the other’s weak points.","52":"Black 49 (S5), White 50 (Q7), Black 51 (R8), White 52 (R9). White’s Q7 cuts in between Black’s R7 and P7, and with Black R8 and White R9 the stones end up mutually cut. On the right side both sides’ stones remain cut, a hard fight in which whoever is left with a weak group first will suffer.","55":"Black 53 (Q8), White 54 (Q6), Black 55 (Q9). Black extends to Q8 and Q9, linking R7 and R8 to Q10 above. White’s Q6 gathers the lower White stones R6 and Q7 into one group. Black’s right-side stones are now one chain, but they are surrounded by White and do not have many liberties.","58":"White 56 (R10), Black 57 (O8), White 58 (Q11). With R10 and Q11 White joins the upper right-side stone (Q12) with R9 and S8 along the edge. Black’s O8 strengthens the central side and blocks White’s way into the centre. White’s right side links up and down, Black links to the centre, and the fight calms for a while.","59":"Black 59 (R4) now invades the bottom-right corner, going into the territory of White’s Q3. It also aims to link with Black’s S5 and S6 above, threatening the footing of White’s R6, Q6 and Q7. For White it becomes a hard choice: protect the corner territory, or protect the stones outside.","63":"White 60 (P6), Black 61 (O6), White 62 (O5), Black 63 (N5). With P6 and O5 White goes to join its right-side stones to those below, and Black presses from above and from the left with O6 and N5. Through O6, Black brings its bottom wall (M5) close to its right-side stones P7 and O8. White chose to protect the stones outside, leaving Black inside the bottom-right corner.","66":"With White 64 (P4) White firmly joins the stones that came down the right side to Q3 below. Black 65 (R2) and White 66 (R3) continue the tussle inside the corner. Black is trying to make room to live in the corner on the low first and second lines.","69":"Black 67 (S3), White 68 (Q2), Black 69 (S2). Black connects its stones along the edge of the bottom-right corner and makes a living shape. White’s bottom-right corner became much smaller than expected, but in return White’s stones are thickly connected outside (P4, P6, Q6). Black cut into territory, White strengthened the outside: a result both sides can accept.","70":"White 70 (N6) cuts in between Black’s O6 and N5, at the seam between Black’s bottom-side and right-side stones. The aim is to split Black’s stones and weaken one part. Black 71 (O7) connects at once, keeping the right-side Black stones together.","74":"After White 72 (M7) and Black 73 (M8), White 74 (L4) pushes in between Black’s K4 and L5 on the bottom side, trying to leave Black’s L3 stranded among White’s bottom stones. Black 75 (N4) and White 76 (N3) follow, and White’s bottom stones join the White stones on the right along the third line.","78":"Black 77 (J4) links Black’s bottom wall to the left, and White 78 (K3) blocks the way out for Black’s L3. The L3 stone now has just one liberty (L2). Black gives it up and puts first the long wall facing the centre: J4, K4, L5, M5, N5, N4. Rather than clinging to a small stone, Black protects the big plan.","79":"Black 79 (L9) is played below and to the right of tengen (K10), making the central Black stones thicker still. Black’s bottom wall, the right-side stones from O6 to Q10, tengen, and the left-side stones from D10 to G10 now ring a huge central framework. White earns territory in the corners and on the sides; the question is how much of the centre Black can turn into territory. The position seems close to even, and the game now enters the middle-game fighting.","80":"White 80 (C6) climbs from the bottom-left White corner up the left side and places a stone on the third line beside Black’s wall. It narrows Black’s left side and will also serve as a foothold for an invasion there later. Black 81 (E4) mends the gap between D4 and F5/G4, making the wall one piece.","82":"White 82 (C10) invades by attaching to the left of Black’s D10, on the edge side. If the whole left side became Black’s territory, Black’s central framework would be very large. White will not allow that and tries to live at the far left end of Black’s formation.","85":"Black 83 (C9), White 84 (D9), Black 85 (C8), White 86 (D8). Black blocks under White’s C10 from the edge side, while White extends D9 and D8 on the inner side of Black’s wall. Each side is pushing the other against the edge. Neither side’s stones can be called alive yet.","88":"Black 87 (C11) is a severe move that cuts between White’s C10 and C12. White 88 (B10) drops towards the edge, widening the space for the C10 stone to live. Black’s aim is to cut off the invading White stones and attack them.","91":"Black 89 (B11), White 90 (D11), Black 91 (E10). Black extends the cutting stone with B11, but White’s D11 presses on the head of Black’s D10 from above. Black’s E10 links D10 towards tengen and protects the left-side wall. Black’s cutting stones B11 and C11 are caught between White stones and running short of liberties.","96":"With White 92 (B12), Black’s two stones B11 and C11 are down to one liberty. Black does not save them; Black 93 (D7) instead blocks under White’s D9 and D8. White 94 (E11), Black 95 (F10) and White 96 (C7) follow, and on the left side both sides are now trying to capture the other’s stones. Black boldly chose capturing over rescuing.","98":"After Black 97 (A9), White 98 (A11) captures the two Black stones B11 and C11. White makes a living shape on the edge of the left side, and Black’s left-side territory is smaller than expected. In return, after Black 99 (E8) White’s D9 and D8 have only one liberty (E9), and Black can capture them whenever it likes. Two stones lost, two stones won: the left side ended roughly even.","100":"White 100 (B4) widens White’s bottom-left corner while pressing under Black’s C4. With B4 and, soon, C5, White’s corner stones C3 and D3 link up with C6 and C7 on the left side. The White stones from the bottom left up the left side become one strong group.","101":"Black 101 (S14) slides from Black’s top-right corner down the right side, reducing the top part of White’s right-side territory (Q12, Q11). While White 102 (C5) secures the bottom left, Black goes one step further with 103 (R13), and White 104 (S12) just about protects the right-side territory. Bit by bit, the borders between the two sides’ territories are being fixed.","105":"Black 105 (K17) is played in the middle of the top side, to the right of White’s H17. It stops White’s top-side territory from spreading further right and works with Black’s top-side stones L15 and N15. The top side looks set for a fight over the border: White on the left, Black on the right.","106":"White 106 (G3) now attaches on the bottom side, right under Black’s G4. White wants to fill the gap between F3 and J3 with its own stone and firm up the bottom-side territory. Black 107 (E3) goes in between White’s D3 and F3 to cut. It may look small, but this exchange decides where the borders of territory lie.","108":"White 108 (E6) is played just above Black’s bottom-left wall, a little above D4 and E4. Working with White’s C5 and C6 on the left side, it pokes at the back of Black’s wall. Black 109 (F6) immediately presses from above, stopping White from coming out into the centre.","113":"White 110 (E2), Black 111 (F2) and White 112 (D2) continue the tussle for stones on the bottom edge, and Black 113 (H3) cuts in between White’s G3 and J3. It tries to break White’s bottom-side stones apart and shrink White’s territory. Black is trying to reduce, even a little, the White territory under its own wall.","116":"Black 115 (H4) connects H3 to the wall, and White 116 (G2) links F3 and G3 from below. Black’s F2 stone is down to one liberty (F1) and is all but captured. Though poked at its gaps, White’s bottom side has held together as one.","117":"Black 117 (F17) invades on the third line of the top side, between White’s top-left enclosure (C16, E16) and White’s H17. It cuts into White’s top-side territory and, if possible, aims to split White’s stones and attack them. A new fight begins on the left half of the top side.","120":"White 118 (J17) strengthens the top-side stones, and Black 119 (D17) goes even deeper into the top-left corner. White 120 (C17) protects the corner. Black is trying to take the very top of White’s corner, around the second line. White guards the corner while getting ready to squeeze Black from outside.","124":"Black 121 (G16), White 122 (H15), Black 123 (G15), White 124 (K15). With G16 and G15 Black extends towards the centre to separate the top-left White stones from White’s H17. With H15 and K15 White links its top-side stones towards the centre and threatens Black’s K17 on the top side. In this top-side fight, Black and White stones keep poking their heads out in turn.","127":"Black 125 (H16), White 126 (J16), Black 127 (H14). Black presses on White’s H15 from above with H16 and from below with H14. The H15 stone is surrounded and has only one liberty left (J15). Black takes the upper centre for itself while weakening White’s stones one by one.","130":"White 128 (L16), Black 129 (M15), White 130 (L17). With L16 and L17 White shuts Black’s K17 in on the top side and gives its top-side stones a living shape. Black’s K17 can hardly move any more. The result: Black gains the upper centre, White keeps the top-side territory.","133":"Black 131 (P13), White 132 (P12), Black 133 (Q13). Black presses the base of White’s right-side stones and cuts White’s top-right stones Q16, Q15 and P14 off from White’s Q12 on the right side. Those top-right White stones are surrounded by Black and in a painful shape. The top-right corner is becoming fairly secure as Black territory.","135":"White 134 (E7) steps from the bottom left into the inside of Black’s wall, and Black 135 (E9) captures White’s two stones D9 and D8. Black had earlier lost two stones on the left side, so this wins back the same number. Black’s left-side stones are now safe, and Black’s territory from the left side into the centre is becoming clearer.","137":"White 136 (F7), Black 137 (G7). White lines up E6, E7 and F7 above Black’s bottom-left wall, trying to come out into the centre, and Black stops it with G7. Black’s central framework is being shaved bit by bit from below too. White does not charge in deeply; its plan is to shrink the edges one by one.","138":"White 138 (N18) goes in on the second line on the right part of the top side, to the left of Black’s P18 and O16. It shrinks Black’s top-right territory from the top-side direction. Later White 148 (O18) pushes further right, and Black’s top-side territory is shaved little by little.","139":"Black 139 (C18) now invades on the second line above White’s top-left corner. Above White’s corner (C16, C17, E16) there is still room for Black to live. If Black lives here, White’s top-left territory will shrink a lot. It is a bold move, trying to live right next to White’s thick stones.","145":"White 140 (E17), Black 141 (E18), White 142 (D16), Black 143 (D18), White 144 (G18), Black 145 (B17). Black lines up C18, D18 and E18 on the second line and goes deep into the corner with B17. White firms up the outside with E17 and D16, and stops Black from spreading right with G18. Above White’s corner, a delicate sequence continues as Black tries to live in a narrow space.","150":"White 146 (B16), Black 147 (B18), White 148 (O18), Black 149 (P19), White 150 (F18). Black gathers its stones above the top-left corner, and White tightens from outside with moves such as F18. White’s top-left corner is now only the part below the line from B16 to E16. Black has kept its stones alive inside White’s area and greatly reduced White’s top-left territory. Meanwhile White has also shaved Black’s territory in the top right with O18, and the fine exchanges go on.","152":"White 152 (G12): White finally goes inside Black’s central framework. G12 lies between White’s left-side stones (E11, D11) and the White stones above, in a gap in Black’s thickness. If White lives here or links up with the White stones around it, Black’s centre will not become territory. From here comes the biggest climax of the game.","153":"Black 153 (K14) is played right under White’s top-side stone K15, blocking the road by which White could link G12 to the top-side stones. At the same time it is a broad road joining Black’s central stones above and below. Black wants to split White into top-side and central parts and attack one of them.","156":"White 154 (J15) rescues White’s H15 on the top side, and after Black 155 (F14) comes White 156 (H11). White puts another stone in the centre and, working with G12, widens its foothold inside Black’s centre. Black’s centre is being seeped into, bit by bit, by White from above and from the left.","159":"White 158 (E5) pokes at the inside of Black’s bottom-left wall. If White went on to play F4, Black’s wall would be cut between E4 and F5. So Black 159 (F4) connects solidly. Before the central fight, Black mends the weak points of its wall one by one.","160":"White 160 (H8): the most famous move of the game. In the wide space at the bottom of Black’s central framework, surrounded by Black stones such as G7, G10, K10 and L9, White places a single stone. This stone is said to aim at several gaps in the links between Black’s stones, and Black cannot guard them all at once. With this move as a trigger, White starts to work its way into Black’s central territory one point after another. A rumour spread that a pupil, not Shusai himself, had found the move, but nothing about that is certain.","161":"Black 161 (J8) is played to the right of H8, stopping White from spreading below tengen, between it and Black’s bottom wall. It is a normal reply that guards the place that looks biggest. But White’s next aim lay somewhere else.","162":"White 162 (K12) now lands above tengen (K10). Working with White’s G12 and H11, it tries to split Black’s centre into an upper and a lower part. A shake-up below with H8, then straight round to the top: each time Black guards one spot, White touches another weak point — a skilful sequence.","165":"Black 163 (F12) is played to the left of G12, trying to separate White’s G12 from the White stones on the left side. White 164 (F11) seeks a connection, and Black 165 (H10) blocks under White’s H11 so that White’s central stones cannot come out downwards. In the centre, Black and White stones are tangled, each trying to cut the other.","168":"White 166 (J14), Black 167 (J12), White 168 (J11). With J14 White extends the top-side stones downwards, and with J11 it links to the central White stone H11. Black’s J12 is a stone that cuts White’s upper and lower parts. The centre has become a very intricate fight in which many stones are cut and connected.","172":"Black 169 (J13), White 170 (K13), Black 171 (K11), White 172 (F13). With F13 White reaches towards the White stones on the left, trying to link its central stones with the White stones on the left side and top left. What used to be Black’s central thickness is turning into a road for White’s stones to pass through.","175":"Black 173 (L14) rescues the single Black stone at K14, then White 174 (J10) and Black 175 (J9). White’s J10 enters right next to tengen on the left, and Black blocks from below with J9. White stones have reached the point just left of tengen (K10), and Black’s central territory keeps getting smaller.","178":"White 176 (H12) connects the central White stones, and after Black 177 (G14) comes White 178 (N9). This time White enters the right part of the centre, between Black’s L9 and O8. Black’s centre has been entered by White from both left and right, and it no longer looks likely to become a big territory. White does not miss Black’s weak points on the other side of the board either.","180":"After Black 179 (D14) protects the top-left Black stones, White 180 (N8) extends further down. With N9 and N8 White tries to separate Black’s right-side stones from O6 to O8 from Black’s central stones L9 and M8. Black’s right-side stones are surrounded by White and short of liberties, and this is becoming a new weak point.","186":"After exchanges on the right edge with Black 181 (T7) and White 182 (T9), Black 183 (N7) blocks under White’s N8; after White 184 (D13) and Black 185 (M11) comes White 186 (P8). It is a sharp move cutting between Black’s P7 and Q8. Black’s five stones O6, O7, O8, P7 and N7 on the right side are down to one liberty (O9), and the five Black stones from R7 to Q10 have only two. With one move White aimed at two Black groups at the same time.","189":"Black 187 (M6) captures White’s N6, and after White 188 (O9), Black 189 (L7) captures White’s M7. Now the five Black stones from O6 to P7 gain liberties and are saved by linking with the Black stones on the bottom side. Black has captured two White stones, but meanwhile, with O9, White has reached round to the other Black group on the right side.","192":"After White 190 (F8) and Black 191 (G8) comes White 192 (P10). Black’s five stones R7, R8, Q8, Q9 and Q10 on the right side are down to a single liberty, P9. Even if Black plays P9 and captures White’s P8, White can play back at P8 at once and capture the whole lot (a “snapback”), so these five stones can no longer be saved. Starting from the cut at White 186, White made a big gain on the right side. Part of the influence Black built towards the centre has become not territory but White’s prey.","197":"After Black 193 (R12), White 194 (S11), Black 195 (C14) and White 196 (E14) comes Black 197 (G11). White’s five central stones G12, H12, H11, J11 and J10 are down to a single liberty, G13. Black wants to capture White in the centre and win back the loss on the right side. Whether White’s central stones survive will decide the game.","198":"White 198 (E12) captures Black’s F12. Now White’s five central stones also have a liberty at F12, and they can link to the White stones on the left either through F12 or through G13. If Black blocks one, White connects through the other, so White’s central stones can no longer be captured. The White stones that split Black’s thickness have survived: this is the scene that decided the game.","203":"With Black 199 (E15), White 200 (E13), Black 201 (D15) and White 202 (B14) the upper left side is settled too, and from Black 203 (B3) the game enters the yose (the endgame stage in which the borders of territory are fixed). Black’s B3 enters White’s bottom-left corner on the first and second lines, trying to shave even a little off White’s territory. From here, fine moves over one or two points follow.","206":"Black 205 (Q1), White 206 (P1), Black 207 (R1), White 208 (P3). On the first line of the bottom-right corner Black shaves a little off White’s territory, and White firmly protects its corner. In the endgame, a single move on the first line makes a difference of one or two points. Even with the big fights over, neither the Meijin nor Go Seigen relaxes.","214":"With Black 209 (B5) and White 210 (A3), White captures Black’s B3; with Black 211 (A6), White 212 (B6), Black 213 (A7) and White 214 (A5), White captures Black’s B5 as well. On the bottom-left edge White takes two stones and fully protects its territory. Black also trims White’s territory higher up the left edge (A6, A7), so each side gains a little.","220":"Black 215 (S13), White 216 (N11), Black 217 (R11), White 218 (M12), Black 219 (L12), White 220 (M10). On the right side of the centre too, White advances with N11, M12 and M10, making the territory left in Black’s centre even smaller. The Black territory that was supposed to spread around tengen is now just a thin strip.","224":"With Black 221 (S10), White 222 (S9), Black 223 (T12) and White 224 (T10), White captures Black’s S10 on the right edge. White’s right-side territory has been completed without giving up a single point. Even when Black tries to win something back, White answers carefully and shows no gap at all.","232":"After Black 225 (B13), White 226 (A15), Black 227 (C13), White 228 (A13), Black 229 (F15), White 230 (G17) and Black 231 (F16) comes White 232 (C15). With this C15, Black’s large group of 18 stones stretching from the left of the top side into the centre is down to a single liberty, G13. While playing endgame moves on the left side, White has created an unexpected danger for Black’s big group. Black must act at once.","233":"Black 233 (L13) captures White’s two stones K12 and K13. Now Black’s big group gains liberties at K12 and K13, links up with the central Black stones and is safe. It was a close call, but by taking these two stones Black protected the big group. Capturing it would have won the game outright for the Meijin, but Go Seigen did not miss the danger.","246":"White 234 (N17), Black 235 (L11), White 236 (F9) … White 244 (G13), Black 245 (F19), White 246 (G19). With G13 White links its central stones to F13, and with F19 and G19 the border on the top edge is settled as well. Only small endgame moves worth one or two points remain. Counting the territory on the board, the difference is tiny.","249":"After White 248 (A16), Black 249 (M13) puts White’s M12 in atari (one move from capture), and White 250 (N12) connects. The last border in the centre is fixed too. Black 251 (R5) tidies up Black’s shape in the bottom right, and only a few moves remain.","252":"With White 252 (M9) the game ends. White won by 2 points. With the new opening of 3-3, star point and tengen, Black, Go Seigen, built great influence in the centre. But the Meijin, Shusai, took solid territory in the corners and on the sides, and in the late middle game, from move 152, he entered Black’s centre. Shaking the centre from move 160 at H8, and turning the cut at P8 on move 186 into the capture of five Black stones on the right side, were decisive, and Black’s central influence did not become as much territory as hoped. In the no-komi era Black, moving first, was thought to have the advantage, so winning with White shows the Meijin’s true strength. On the other hand, by playing the Meijin to within just 2 points at 19, Go Seigen made the New Opening leave a deep mark on the go world. Later, in ten-game matches, Go Seigen beat the top players of the day one after another and became the leading player of the Showa era."}},"century1956":{"t":"The Game of the Century","w":"Donald Byrne","b":"Bobby Fischer","ev":"The third Rosenwald Trophy tournament (Rosenwald Memorial)","d":"1956","pl":"United States (New York)","lead":"Thirteen-year-old Bobby Fischer beats Donald Byrne, one of America's strongest masters. On move 17 Fischer lets his queen be taken for nothing, then wins piece after piece back, and at the end his small pieces hunt down White's king. Fischer later became world champion.","c":{"0":"October 17, 1956: a game from the Rosenwald Trophy tournament (the Rosenwald Memorial), held at the Marshall Chess Club in New York. It was a strong event for invited top American players, and Bobby Fischer, playing Black, was only 13 years old, the youngest player there. White is Donald Byrne, 26, winner of the 1953 U.S. Open and one of the leading American masters of the time. Fischer’s overall result in the tournament was modest, but this one game made his name known around the world. In the magazine Chess Review, Hans Kmoch called it “The Game of the Century”, and the name is still used today. Let’s follow it move by move with Musowl and see how a 13-year-old boy beat a grown master.","1":"White plays the knight to f3. It controls the central squares e5 and d4 and helps guard the king’s side: a very calm start. White does not commit any pawns yet, so many different set-ups are still possible.","2":"Fischer also plays his knight to f6. It controls e4 and d5. Like White, he keeps his plans flexible and waits to see what his opponent does.","3":"White plays c4. This queen’s-side pawn attacks the d5 square from the side. The idea is to make it harder for Black to put a pawn on d5 and to gain space in the centre.","4":"Black plays g6. Next the bishop will go to g7 and look at the centre along the long diagonal. Fischer loved this kind of set-up from a young age: it holds back at first and then strikes back hard.","5":"White brings the other knight to c3. It controls d5 and e4 even more, and later White can line up pawns in the centre with d4 and e4.","6":"The bishop goes to g7. For now it stands behind the knight on f6, but as soon as the knight moves, the long diagonal from h8 to a1 opens up. Black is also ready to castle.","7":"White plays d4. With pawns on c4 and d4, White takes the centre. If Black answers with d5, the game becomes an opening called the Grünfeld Defence.","8":"Black castles. The king moves to safety on g8, and the rook on f8 comes closer to the centre. Young Fischer makes his king safe first, before the fighting starts.","9":"White plays the bishop to f4. It eyes c7 and b8 along the diagonal and also controls e5. But White’s king is still in the middle on e1, and White is not yet ready to castle.","10":"Black plays d5 and hits the pawn on c4 in the centre. Now it is a Grünfeld Defence. Black’s plan is to let White have the centre for a while and then attack it later.","11":"White’s queen comes out early to b3. It puts more pressure on d5 and also aims at the pawn on b7 down the b-file. It is a strong move, but a queen that comes out early can easily be chased around by enemy pieces.","12":"Black takes the pawn on c4. The pawn that captured now attacks the queen on b3 diagonally, so White has to take it back. In this way Black removes one of White’s central pawns.","13":"White’s queen recaptures on c4. The queen now stands near the middle of the board and looks at d5 and f7. But this queen will be chased by Black’s pieces again and again.","14":"Black plays c6. It controls d5 and b5, so White’s queen and knight cannot go there. It also prepares b5 later to chase the queen.","15":"White plays e4. Two pawns, on d4 and e4, now stand side by side in the centre. Just from the look of it, White has lots of space and seems to be playing comfortably.","16":"Black plays the knight to d7. From here it plans to jump to b6 and chase the queen. It also helps prepare the pawn move e5 to hit White’s centre.","17":"White plays the rook to d1. It supports the pawn on d4 from behind and makes the d-file strong. But because the rook moved before White castled, the king is still stuck in the middle on e1.","18":"The knight jumps to b6 and attacks the queen on c4. White’s queen has to move again. Black is bringing pieces forward while making White waste moves.","19":"The queen escapes to c5. From c5 it eyes the pawn on e7 and also tries to hold back Black’s queen’s side. But the queen is now far forward on the board, in a place where Black’s pieces can easily attack it.","20":"The bishop goes to g4 and attacks the knight on f3. Behind the knight is the rook on d1, so if the knight moves, the rook will be taken: this is called a pin. The knight on f3 guards important squares like d4 and e5, so now White can hardly move it.","21":"White plays the bishop to g5. It attacks the knight on f6 and tries to stop Black from moving that knight and playing e5. But White still has pieces at home and has not castled. Many people say it would have been better to play the bishop to e2 and get ready to castle.","22":"The knight goes to a4! This is the first big surprise of the game. The knight on a4 attacks both the queen on c5 and the knight on c3. If White takes it with the c3 knight, Black takes the pawn on e4 with the f6 knight and attacks the queen on c5 and the bishop on g5 at the same time. White’s king would still be in the middle under attack, so White cannot take the knight. It is said that, after this move, people gathered around the board to watch.","23":"White does not take the knight and moves the queen to a3 instead. From there it still eyes the pawn on e7 along the diagonal. White picked the way of defending that does the least damage.","24":"The knight takes the knight on c3. That knight was the piece that guarded the pawn on e4. So Black has now removed the defender of e4.","25":"White recaptures with a pawn. The material is equal, but White’s pawns on c3 and d4 are now a little loose. And nothing is guarding the pawn on e4 any more.","26":"The knight takes the pawn on e4! From e4 it attacks the pawn on c3 and the bishop on g5. White’s neat pawns in the centre have broken up, the e-file is opening, and the space in front of White’s king in the middle is now wide open.","27":"White takes the pawn on e7 with the bishop. From e7 it attacks Black’s queen on d8 and the rook on f8 at the same time: a double attack. White wins back the pawn and attacks Black’s big pieces. A strong counterattack.","28":"Black moves the queen to b6. It is a bold move that says: you can have the rook on f8. If White takes the rook, Black recaptures with the bishop, and that bishop then chases the queen on a3. Black thinks that White’s king still being in the middle matters more than a rook. The queen on b6 also eyes the pawn on d4.","29":"White does not take the rook and brings the bishop to c4 instead. This empties f1, so White can castle next move and take the king to safety. White also knows that king safety is the biggest problem.","30":"The knight takes the pawn on c3! From here it attacks the rook on d1. If White takes this knight with the queen, Black can bring a rook to e8 and attack the bishop on e7 and the king behind it in a straight line. Black’s pieces are gathering one after another around White’s king.","31":"White plays the bishop to c5 and attacks Black’s queen on b6. If Black simply moves the queen away, White can castle next move. But because the bishop left e7, the e-file is now open for Black’s rook.","32":"The rook moves to e8: check along the e-file! The bishop on e7 has gone to c5, so the e-file was open all the way from e8 to e1. Before saving the queen, Black first goes after White’s king.","33":"The king goes to f1. Now White can never castle. The rook on h1 is also stuck behind the king and has trouble getting out. Black has gained a moment, but the queen on b6 is still attacked by the bishop on c5.","34":"The bishop goes to e6! This is the most famous move of the game: Black lets the queen be taken for nothing. The bishop on e6 attacks White’s bishop on c4, and taking it will be check. If White takes on e6 with the c4 bishop, Black checks with the queen from b5, keeps checking with the knight, and at the end the king is surrounded by its own pieces and cannot move: a “smothered mate”. Fischer wrote that moving the knight to b5 instead would have lost to White taking the pawn on f7. A 13-year-old boy had looked far ahead, well past giving up his queen.","35":"White takes Black’s queen. If you only count pieces, White is far ahead. But now Black’s pieces will chase White’s king and win back piece after piece.","36":"The bishop takes White’s bishop on c4, with check! From c4 it aims through d3 and e2 at the king on f1. Black’s counterattack begins here.","37":"The king escapes to g1. Black’s rook controls the e-file, so there is almost nowhere else to go. On g1 the king is shut in by the rook on h1 and the pawn on g2 and can hardly move.","38":"The knight jumps to e2 with check. The king has to go back to f1. Black ties White’s hands with checks while getting ready for the next capture.","39":"The king goes back to f1. Now the knight on e2 stands in front of the bishop on c4. If this knight moves, the bishop behind it will give check to the king on f1: a “discovered check”.","40":"The knight takes the pawn on d4: discovered check! Because the knight moved, the bishop on c4 now checks the king on f1. When you are in check, you must deal with the king first, no matter what the knight is doing.","41":"The king goes to g1. White keeps getting checked, so there is no time to do anything else.","42":"The knight jumps to e2 again with check. The king has to go back to f1. Black can give check again and again, and each time looks for a chance to win something.","43":"The king goes to f1 again. White can only repeat the same moves. This pattern of checking, grabbing a piece and coming back is called a “windmill”, because it goes round and round.","44":"The knight moves to c3: another discovered check! The bishop on c4 checks the king on f1, and at the same time the knight on c3 attacks the rook on d1. White is in check, so the rook cannot be saved. The windmill is ready to grab another piece.","45":"The king goes to g1. Black could keep checking, but here Black stops for a moment to win back White’s bishop on b6.","46":"The pawn takes the bishop on b6. Now the bishop that took Black’s queen is gone too. Because the a7 pawn moved, the a-file is open, and the rook on a8 attacks White’s queen on a3. Count the pieces: Black has two rooks, two bishops and a knight; White has a queen, two rooks and a knight. Black can also take the rook on d1 next, so Black is getting back more than the queen was worth.","47":"White’s queen escapes to b4. From here it attacks the bishop on c4, the knight on c3 and the pawn on b6 all at once: a sharp escape. White is trying hard to win something back.","48":"The rook goes to a4! It chases the queen again. The rook on a4 is guarded by the knight on c3, and once the queen moves away, it also guards the bishop on c4. White’s queen cannot take any of Black’s pieces and has to run.","49":"White’s queen takes the pawn on b6. At least White wins back one pawn. But meanwhile it is the c3 knight’s turn to take the rook on d1.","50":"The knight takes the rook on d1. Black has two rooks, two bishops and a knight against White’s queen, rook and knight, and is clearly ahead. White’s rook on h1 is still shut in.","51":"White plays h3. This makes an escape square on h2 for the king. Once the king goes to h2, the rook on h1 can finally come out. It also stops Black from threatening White’s back rank forever.","52":"The rook takes the pawn on a2. White has no pawns left on the queen’s side, and nothing can stop Black’s pawns there any more. The rook has entered White’s second rank, where it can also aim at f2 near White’s king.","53":"The king moves to h2. Now the rook on h1 can finally move freely along the first rank. White is trying somehow to bring the remaining pieces into the game.","54":"The knight takes the pawn on f2. From f2 it attacks the rook on h1 and the pawn on h3. Another pawn near White’s king is gone.","55":"White moves the rook to e1 to escape and offers to trade it for Black’s rook on the e-file. The idea is to remove one of Black’s attacking pieces.","56":"Black trades rooks. When you have more pieces, trading usually helps you. Before taking back on e1 with the knight, White first gives check with the queen.","57":"The queen moves to d8 with check. With the rook on e8 gone, the eighth rank was open all the way to Black’s king on g8. White puts in this check first and plans to take back the rook afterwards.","58":"Black blocks the check with the bishop on f8. The bishop comes next to the king, and the defence becomes solid.","59":"The knight takes back the rook on e1. White has a queen and a knight; Black has a rook, two bishops and a knight. Adding up the strength of the pieces, Black is clearly better.","60":"The bishop goes to d5. It eyes the f3 square and the pawn on g2, and from behind along the diagonal it also guards the rook on a2. Black is gathering the pieces one by one on good squares where they support each other.","61":"The knight goes back to f3. Guarded by the pawn on g2, it is the only piece defending White’s king. With the queen and knight, White is trying to hold out against Black’s attack.","62":"The knight goes to e4. It steps into the centre and comes closer to White’s king. Next Black wants to play the f8 bishop to d6 and give check to the king on h2.","63":"White’s queen goes to b8. It attacks the pawn on b7 and, along the eighth rank, pins the f8 bishop to the king on g8. If the bishop moved, the king would be in check, so this stops Black’s plan of checking with the bishop.","64":"Black plays b5. The pawn moves two squares and escapes from the queen’s attack. On b5 it is guarded by the pawn on c6. One day Black may also push this pawn forward.","65":"White plays h4. The h-pawn goes forward, hoping to break up the pawns around Black’s king with h5 if possible. White does not have much left to do.","66":"Black plays h5 too. This stops White’s pawn and keeps the shape around Black’s king solid. Black is in no hurry and plays safely.","67":"The knight goes to e5. From the centre it attacks f7. Also, if the knight jumped to d7, the pinned bishop on f8 would be attacked by two pieces, the queen and the knight. This is White’s last hope of a counterattack.","68":"The king goes to g7. It frees the f8 bishop from the pin and also guards it. Now the bishop can move freely. Black is ready for the final attack.","69":"The king goes to g1. On h2 it could be checked by the freed bishop from d6, so it seems White stepped away first. But there are almost no defending pieces near White’s king.","70":"The bishop moves to c5: check! From c5 it aims through d4, e3 and f2 at the king on g1. Now Black’s small pieces begin to hunt White’s king.","71":"The king goes to f1. White’s king looks for a way out towards the left side of the board. But Black’s rook and bishops are waiting there, and the king is slowly driven to the other side of the board.","72":"The knight jumps to g3 with check. The king is driven to e1. The knight also blocks the king’s way back to the right side of the board.","73":"The king goes to e1. It cannot go back to f2 or g1, so it can only run to the left. Black’s net is slowly getting tighter.","74":"The other bishop comes out to b4 with check. From b4 it aims through c3 and d2 at e1. The king is driven further left, to d1.","75":"The king goes to d1. White’s queen and knight are on the far side of the board and cannot come to help the king.","76":"The bishop moves from d5 to b3 with check. From b3 it aims through c2 at d1. This bishop will later have an important job: guarding the square of the final rook move.","77":"The king goes to c1. There are almost no squares left to run to. One by one, Black’s pieces are closing the squares around White’s king.","78":"The knight jumps to e2 with check. From e2 it attacks c1. The king can only escape to b1.","79":"The king goes to b1. It has been chased almost into the corner of the board.","80":"The knight jumps to c3 with check. From c3 it attacks b1 and also controls d1. The king has to go back to c1.","81":"The king goes to c1. This will be its last square. The knight on c3 covers b1 and d1, and the rook that is about to arrive will cover b2 and d2.","82":"The rook moves to c2: checkmate! The rook on c2 is guarded by the bishop on b3, so the king cannot take it; the knight on c3 covers b1 and d1, and the rook covers b2 and d2. Three pieces, a rook, a bishop and a knight, make the mating net, and the other bishop helped chase the king here. White’s king was hunted from g1 all the way to c1 on the other side of the board, and White’s queen could do nothing to the end. Byrne could have resigned earlier, but it is said he played on to checkmate out of respect for the boy’s brilliant play. The knight leap on move 11, the decision to give up the queen on move 17, the “windmill” that won the pieces back, and a mating net woven by small pieces: after this game, Fischer became U.S. champion at 14 and world champion in 1972."}},"oshiro1849":{"t":"Shusaku’s First Castle Game","w":"Honinbo Shusaku","b":"Yasui Sanchi IX","ev":"Castle Game (the yearly game at Edo Castle)","d":"1849","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"The Castle Games were the yearly showcase where players of the official go houses played at the shogun’s castle. Twenty-year-old Shusaku made his debut this year and won with Black by 11 points — the start of a perfect record of 19 wins in 19 Castle Games.","c":{"0":"1849. The year before, Shusaku had been named heir to the Honinbo house, and now he plays his first Castle Game. It is said that such games were often played beforehand elsewhere and only replayed at the castle.","1":"Black 1 on the 3-4 point at top right (R16). As usual, Shusaku starts with a calm 3-4 point.","4":"White 4 (C15) approaches Black’s D17 in the top left. A fight starts there early on.","5":"Black 5 (C11) pincers the approaching white stone from below, so White cannot settle easily on the left side.","12":"White 12 (C17). White takes the top-left corner and Black gets stones facing outwards — a fair trade for both.","13":"Black 13 (J17) makes use of the outside stones by extending along the top.","15":"Black 15 (C5) takes the last empty corner, at bottom left. All four corners are now occupied.","16":"White 16 (E4) approaches that black stone at once, and a fight starts at bottom left too.","19":"Black 19 (D2) firmly secures the bottom-left corner — a solid, Shusaku-like choice.","23":"Black 23 (E5) comes out between White’s stones and strengthens Black’s group at bottom left.","29":"Black 29 (F11) reinforces Black’s stones on the left while also looking towards the centre.","30":"White 30 (H4) extends along the bottom, pressing Black’s bottom-left group while building White’s own area.","31":"Black 31 (O3) approaches White’s bottom-right corner.","33":"Black 33 (R3) attaches to White’s corner stone. From here Black builds on the outside while White crawls along the third line at the edge.","43":"Black 43 (Q8). A thick black wall now runs down the right side. White has taken the corner and edge territory; Black has gained outside strength.","44":"White 44 (L17) steps in between Black’s stones on the top side so Black’s area does not grow too large.","50":"White 50 (R18) slips under Black’s top-right corner, aiming at the corner territory.","51":"Black 51 (P13). With the wall behind it, Black frames a large area from the right side towards the centre.","52":"White 52 (D10) jumps in between Black’s stones on the left side. Black and White stones become tangled there.","63":"Black 63 (B10) plays at the left edge so that White’s stones cannot make a base there.","65":"Black 65 (G3) also goes into White’s area on the bottom side, reducing White’s territory.","73":"Black 73 (K17) firms up Black’s top-side stones and puts pressure on White’s stone of move 44.","76":"White 76 (P11) jumps into Black’s right-side area. Whether this stone lives or dies will make a big difference to the score.","77":"Black 77 (Q11) answers from the side of its wall, pushing White out towards the centre — calm defence that also attacks, typical of Shusaku.","80":"White 80 (N10) runs into the centre, trying to escape Black’s pursuit.","85":"Black 85 (N12) keeps attacking while expanding its own territory on the top and right. Gaining profit by attacking — skilful play.","91":"Black 91 (J7) plays above White’s bottom stones, capping White’s area from the centre as well.","100":"White 100 (S17) tries something in the top-right corner to reduce Black’s corner territory.","119":"Black 119 (R17) defends the top-right corner firmly and holds off White’s attempt.","122":"White 122 (G6) goes around the outside of Black’s bottom-left stones, trying to cut them off from Black’s centre.","131":"Black 131 (M5) drives a wedge into White’s area in the middle of the bottom side.","140":"White 140 (B4) tries something in Black’s bottom-left corner. A close capturing fight begins along the left edge.","156":"White 156 (A8) captures two black stones. The fight on the left edge settles after both sides take a little.","158":"White 158 (L10). White still has stones in the centre and tries to reduce Black’s central territory.","162":"White 162 (O12) captures a black stone. Even so, Black’s right-side area remains large.","172":"White 172 (K18). From here it is the endgame, starting with the border on the top side.","186":"White 186 (T16) trims Black’s territory at the top-right edge. In the endgame, both sides play the biggest points first.","205":"Black 205 (Q18) closes off the right part of the top side and fixes the territory there.","223":"Black 223 (P10) fixes the border between Black’s right-side territory and the white stones that escaped into the centre.","233":"Black 233 (Q9) captures a white stone. Black’s right-side territory is now almost entirely safe.","239":"Black 239 (A6) captures a stone and a ko appears at the left edge. The fight over single points goes on to the very end.","260":"White 260 (T6) captures a stone, making another ko at the bottom-right edge. Black takes back at 263 (T5).","267":"The game ends after 267 moves: Black wins by 11 points. No wild adventures — Shusaku simply attacked and let his territory grow naturally, a very Shusaku-like win. It was the first of his 19 straight Castle Game victories."}},"shilin":{"t":"The Shilin Guangji game","w":"Red (name not recorded)","b":"Black (name not recorded)","ev":"The everyday encyclopedia Shilin Guangji: “a game won after giving the first move, with same-side cannons”","d":"13th century","pl":"China (Song–Yuan)","lead":"This record comes from the Shilin Guangji, an everyday encyclopedia compiled by Chen Yuanjing around the end of the Song dynasty and the start of the Yuan. It is said to be one of the oldest complete xiangqi game records that survive. It is a model game in which the stronger side lets the other move first, and in only nine moves Black’s horse traps Red’s chariot.","c":{"0":"This record is more than 700 years old, from the time between the Song and Yuan dynasties. It appears in the games chapter of the encyclopedia Shilin Guangji, and it is said to be one of the oldest complete xiangqi games we still have. The players’ names are not recorded. The title says the stronger player “gave the first move” to the weaker one, so this is a lesson showing how Red, who moves first, loses. Red is at the bottom and starts.","1":"Red moves the right cannon to the middle file. This is the Central Cannon, aimed straight at Black’s general, and still one of the strongest openings today. It shows that this opening was already played 700 years ago.","2":"Black answers with a cannon to the middle too, so the two cannons face each other. When both sides move the cannon on the same side, it is called Same Direction Cannons. Cannon against cannon has been a popular fight for centuries.","3":"Red jumps a horse out to guard the middle soldier. That soldier is under attack from Black’s cannon, so it needs a defender. The horse is also getting ready to come forward.","4":"Black does the same and guards the middle soldier with a horse. Both sides are building a firm base first.","5":"Red moves the right chariot one step sideways onto an open file. The chariot is the strongest piece on the board, so it is good to get it working early.","6":"Black brings out the other horse and leaves the chariots at home for now. The plan is to bring pieces forward first. But a chariot that comes out late can be hard to use in the attack later.","7":"Red’s chariot rushes across the river into Black’s camp. It wants to threaten Black’s pieces, but Red’s other pieces are still at home, so this attack is a bit hasty. Bringing out the left horse or chariot first was also possible.","8":"Black pushes the soldier on file 3 one step. This opens a path for the horse so it can jump forward later. Black is almost inviting Red’s chariot to come.","9":"Red’s chariot takes a soldier and now eyes the black horse beyond it. Winning a piece and threatening another looks great. But the chariot has run far ahead with no friends around it. A chariot alone in the enemy camp is easy to chase.","10":"Black’s horse jumps forward. It escapes from the chariot and moves toward Red’s camp at the same time — two jobs with one move. This horse will be the star of the game.","11":"Red pulls the chariot back one step to attack the horse from the side. A chariot can capture from far away, so the horse cannot rest. Still, this move does not help Red develop any other piece.","12":"Black’s horse goes further forward and crosses the river into Red’s camp. Each time it runs from the chariot, it gets closer to Red’s general.","13":"Red pushes a soldier to open a path for Red’s own horse. The idea is to bring that horse out and trade it for Black’s horse. But this gives Black time to go even further.","14":"Black lifts an elephant and attacks Red’s chariot. Using a defending piece to chase the enemy chariot away is very efficient. Red must move the chariot again, and Black gains another move.","15":"Red’s chariot slides across and takes another soldier. Red is now two soldiers up, but this is the move that loses. The chariot is now far from its general and cannot come back to defend. Red needed a move to guard against the horse, such as bringing the chariot home.","16":"Black’s horse jumps deeper and attacks Red’s chariot. And there is more: if the horse jumps once again, it will check Red’s general and at the same time attack the chariot in Red’s corner. One horse has made two threats at once.","17":"Red chases the horse with the chariot. But the horse can no longer be stopped. Around Red’s general there is nobody left to help.","18":"The horse leaps in with check! It also attacks the chariot in Red’s corner, so when the general steps away, the chariot will be lost. Only nine moves, but the game teaches what happens to a chariot that runs ahead alone, and why pieces should fight together. Players 700 years ago were thinking about the same things on the same board."}},"qima13":{"t":"The Thirteen-Move Horse Sacrifice (Juzhongmi)","w":"Red (name not recorded)","b":"Black (name not recorded)","ev":"Juzhongmi, Volume 1, Game 1","d":"1632 (published)","pl":"China (Ming)","lead":"This is the very first game in the Juzhongmi (“The Secret in the Tangerine”), a xiangqi book published by Zhu Jinzhen of the Ming dynasty in 1632. Red lets Black capture a horse for free, rushes in while Black is busy, and mates in thirteen moves. Under the name “The Thirteen-Move Horse Sacrifice” it is still loved almost 400 years later.","c":{"0":"This is Game 1 of the Juzhongmi, a book printed in 1632, near the end of the Ming dynasty. Together with the later Plum Blossom Manual, it is called one of the two great classics of xiangqi. This is not a real match but a model game written to teach a plan, so there are no players’ names. Red is at the bottom and moves first, and the game is over in just thirteen moves.","1":"Red moves the right cannon to the middle. This is the Central Cannon, staring straight at Black’s general. The Juzhongmi is famous for studying how to attack with this opening.","2":"Black also moves the cannon on the same side to the middle. This is Same Direction Cannons, where the cannons clash head-on. Each side is aiming at the other’s middle soldier.","3":"Red jumps a horse out to guard the middle soldier. It protects the soldier the black cannon is aiming at, and the horse starts to work too.","4":"Black also guards the middle soldier with a horse. So far both sides have the same shape.","5":"Red moves the right chariot just one step forward. Now it can run sideways next move. A chariot that moves sideways like this is called a “horizontal chariot”, and it can quickly swing to either wing.","6":"Black moves the chariot one step sideways so it can drive straight up the board. This is a “straight chariot”. Horizontal chariot against straight chariot — two different ways of using the chariot face off.","7":"Red’s horizontal chariot swings over to a file on the left. That file runs right beside Black’s advisor, where Black’s defence is thin. Red is getting ready to break in from here.","8":"Black’s straight chariot crosses the river and goes deep into Red’s camp. Black wants to fight back with an attack of its own. But it is a move that hurries to attack before defending.","9":"Red’s chariot drives deep into Black’s camp. It stands on the elephant’s “eye”, the point an elephant must pass through, so Black’s elephant cannot move. Right next to the advisor, it is now very close to the general.","10":"Black brings the other horse out to the edge. Part of the idea is to stop Red’s chariot from sliding in sideways.","11":"Here is the horse sacrifice! Red lifts the other chariot one step. This leaves the horse in the left corner with no defender, so Black’s cannon can take it for free. But in return for one horse, Red gains time to bring both chariots into the attack quickly.","12":"Black’s cannon takes Red’s horse. It looks like Black has won a piece. But this cannon is now far from home and cannot help defend. It is said that strengthening the defence by moving up an advisor, instead of capturing, was better here.","13":"Red’s cannon moves forward. Using Black’s middle cannon as a “screen” to jump over, it now aims at Black’s horse beyond it. The cannon is a strange piece that captures by jumping over exactly one piece.","14":"Black retreats the horse to escape. But that horse was the important piece guarding the middle soldier. By running away, it leaves a hole in Black’s centre. Black had other defences, but this was the worst choice.","15":"Red’s cannon takes the middle soldier — check! With Black’s horse gone, the centre was wide open. From here Red’s attack rushes forward.","16":"Black moves an advisor up to block in front of the general. This is the only good way to stop the check.","17":"Red’s second chariot arrives on the same file. Two chariots lined up on one file are extremely strong. The plan is to break into the back of Black’s camp.","18":"Black’s general steps to the side. Otherwise it would be stared at straight down the file by Red’s two chariots.","19":"Red’s front chariot takes Black’s advisor — check! This chariot will be captured by the other advisor at once, but that is part of the plan. Red throws away a chariot to smash the guards around Black’s general. What a bold move!","20":"Black takes the chariot with the advisor. In pieces, Black is now far ahead. But the general’s house is even emptier than before.","21":"Red’s last chariot moves to the general’s file — check again. Black’s general has almost no pieces left to protect it.","22":"Black puts a cannon in front of the general to block the check. Putting a piece in between like this is called interposing.","23":"Red’s chariot takes that cannon — check again. The chariot keeps capturing Black’s pieces while chasing the general into a corner.","24":"Black’s general can only go back to the middle. Every other point is covered by Red’s pieces.","25":"Finally Red’s cannon lines up in the middle, two cannons on one file — check. The front cannon is the screen and the back cannon hits the general: a “double cannon” mate. Black’s general has nowhere to go, and Red wins. By giving up just one horse, Red won in thirteen moves. For 400 years this game has been teaching that time and active pieces can matter more than the number of pieces."}},"sokei1607":{"t":"The oldest shogi record that survives","w":"Ōhashi Sōkei (I)","b":"Hon'inbō Sansa","ev":"The oldest game on record","d":"June 1607","pl":"Japan (the place is not recorded)","lead":"Of all shogi games written down move by move, this is the oldest one we still have. Sente is Sōkei, who later founded the Ōhashi house of shogi masters. Gote is Sansa, founder of the Hon'inbō house of Go, who was also a strong shogi player. Sōkei wins in 133 moves.","c":{"0":"Welcome, I am Musowl. This game was played in 1607, just as the Edo period was beginning. Sente, Ōhashi Sōkei, would later be called the first Meijin, the top shogi master of the land. Gote, Hon'inbō Sansa, founded the Hon'inbō house of Go and was very good at shogi too; the two often played in front of important people. Eight of their even games survive, and Sōkei is said to have won seven of them. More than 400 years later, we can still replay every move. 1607 was the fourth year after Tokugawa Ieyasu set up his government in Edo. Having handed the title of shogun to his son Hidetada, Ieyasu began building a castle in Sunpu (today's Shizuoka City) that very year. This game comes from the moment Japan was moving from the age of warring states into the Edo period.","1":"P-7f. This opens the bishop's diagonal, and it is still the most common first move today. So shogi already began this way 400 years ago.","2":"P-3d. Gote opens the bishop's diagonal too. Now the two bishops face each other across the middle of the board. Sansa was born in Kyoto in 1559 and became a Buddhist monk at the age of eight. \"Hon'inbō\" was the name of the small hall he lived in at Jakkōji temple, and it became the name of his Go house. It is said that Oda Nobunaga once praised him as a true master.","3":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver. It is getting ready to bring the silver forward. Sōkei was born around 1555 and is said to have come from a family of Kyoto townspeople. In 1603 he presented a book of tsume-shogi problems he had made to the Emperor. It is said to be the oldest tsume-shogi collection on record.","5":"P-4f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 4 to make room for the silver. It goes together with S-4g next.","6":"S-4b. Gote also lifts a silver. Defenders are gathering on Gote's file 4. Five years after this game, in 1612, Ieyasu began paying the best Go and shogi players a stipend in rice. This was the start of the \"iemoto\" houses that made shogi their profession, and Sōkei's Ōhashi house went on for the whole Edo period.","10":"R-4b. Gote moves the rook to file 4. Today this is called the Fourth File Rook, one of the Ranging Rook plans where the rook swings to the left. People were already playing it 400 years ago. So this game is what we now call an \"opposing\" game: a Static Rook Sente against a Ranging Rook Gote. Four hundred years later, the Fourth File Rook is still a favourite of both professionals and amateurs. It is fun to see a popular plan already in the very oldest record.","11":"R-4h. Sente's rook goes to file 4 too, so both rooks face each other there. Sente will move this rook many more times, looking for a good place to attack. In today's words, Sente's rook stands on the \"Right Fourth File Rook\" square. The rook faces Gote's Fourth File Rook head-on and attacks straight up file 4, and it is still played today as one way to break the Fourth File Rook.","12":"B-3c. The bishop steps up to add strength to file 4. Gote has made file 4 very solid.","16":"K-6b. Gote moves the king to the left. In Ranging Rook, the king usually hides on the side away from the rook. The farther the king is from the fighting, the safer it is.","17":"K-6h. Sente moves the king to the left as well. It will go on to 7h and then 8g. In those days, named castles such as today's Mino Castle had probably not yet taken a clear shape. You can see both players feeling their way as they guard their kings.","19":"K-7h. The king reaches file 7. The next goal is to gather golds and silvers around it to build a castle.","21":"G-5h. The right gold comes toward the king. The gold is a great defender, and having one close by makes the king feel safe.","24":"S-5c. Gote brings a silver to file 5. With silvers on 5c and 4c, the center is solid too.","29":"S-6g. Sente's silver arrives near the king. With the silver on 6g and the gold on 7h, a wall is forming around the king.","30":"G-6c. Gote lifts a gold to thicken the front of the king. Both players are building their camps slowly and carefully.","33":"P-8f. Sente pushes the pawn on file 8 to make a place for the king. It is preparing K-8g.","35":"K-8g. The king goes to 8g. It is not a castle we use today: the king sits high, near the edge. We can see how players of that time thought about defence.","36":"K-8b. Gote's king settles on 8b. Each king is now tucked away deep in its own corner.","39":"N-7g. The knight jumps up so that it can leap to 6e at any time. Sente wants the castle's pieces to help in the attack too.","41":"R-3h. Sente shifts the rook to file 3. Gote's file 4 is too strong, so Sente is changing the place to attack. At that time there were no books yet that wrote down the set ways to play the opening (joseki). Masters were still finding good shapes one game at a time. Later players of the Ōhashi and Itō houses studied records like this one and grew the joseki.","42":"R-3b. Gote at once moves its rook to file 3 as well. Wherever Sente's rook goes, Gote's rook follows.","43":"P-2e. Now Sente pushes the pawn on file 2. The plan is to bring the rook to file 2 and attack there.","44":"B-5a. Gote pulls the bishop back. It rearranges its pieces so that other pieces can meet an attack on file 2.","45":"R-2h. The rook is back on its starting file 2. File 4, file 3, file 2: Sente's rook has made a long journey.","46":"R-2b. Gote's rook again follows to file 2. Wherever Sente sends its rook, Gote meets it on the same file. This 'rook chase' is the highlight of the opening of this game.","47":"P-6e. Now Sente starts the fight on file 6, where no rook stands. Instead of attacking where the rooks stare at each other, Sente strikes where Gote is thinner. The middle game begins. Starting a fight on a file where the enemy rook is not is an idea still used in opposing games today. The master of 400 years ago had already noticed it.","49":"Px6d. Sente pushes the pawn and takes. The idea is to lure Gote's gold forward and loosen the defence around the king.","50":"Gx6d. Gote takes back with the gold. The gold has moved a little away from its king, so a small gap opens in Gote's defence.","51":"P-4e. Next Sente pushes the pawn on file 4. It shakes Gote's defence on file 4 as well.","53":"N-3g. The other knight jumps up to join the attack. Sente's attackers are moving forward one by one.","54":"P*6e. Gote drops a pawn on 6e, the square where Sente's knight wants to jump. A pawn is cheap, but it is very useful for blocking an enemy piece's path.","56":"P-7e. Gote strikes back. It pokes at the file right above Sente's king. If the pawns above a king are broken, the king becomes hard to defend.","57":"Px7e. Sente takes with the pawn. If not, Gote's pawn would push on to 7f.","58":"Gx7e. Gote takes back with the gold, which now stands on 7e. Gote is bringing the gold forward to press on Sente's king.","59":"P*7f. Sente drops a pawn to block the gold. This pawn guards the head of the king.","61":"R-6i. Sente's rook travels all the way to the bottom of file 6. It wants to add the rook's power to the fight on file 6. Later this rook will be taken by Gote's silver.","63":"Nx6e. The knight from 7g jumps and takes the pawn on 6e. The knight Sente prepared long ago finally gets to work.","64":"Nx6e. Gote takes back with its own knight. The knights are traded.","66":"P-8e. Gote presses on file 8 too. It tries to strip away the pawns in front of Sente's king one by one.","68":"P*7g. Gote drops a pawn on 7g, right next to the king. If Sente takes it, the taking piece is pulled out of place; if not, the pawn will promote. It is a common trick to break a defence with a small pawn.","69":"G-7i. Sente steps the gold back out of the way. It chooses to keep its defensive shape instead of taking the pawn now.","70":"N*7e, check. Gote drops a knight. The knight reaches the king on 8g by jumping over other pieces. Gote's attack is getting strong.","72":"Sx7e. After Sente's pawn takes the knight, Gote takes back with the silver and moves forward. Gote's attacking pieces are now right next to Sente's king.","74":"P*8f, check. Gote drops another pawn right in front of the king on 8g. The king cannot stay there any longer.","75":"K-9h. Sente's king escapes to 9h on the edge. It steps away from danger and rebuilds its defence.","77":"Px7e. Now Sente's pawn on 7f takes the silver on 7e. Sente removes the centre of Gote's attack and gets a chance to strike back.","79":"S*7f. Sente drops a silver to clash with the gold on 7e. It guards the king's head and tries to drive the gold away.","81":"Sx7f. Silver and gold have been traded. Sente is cutting down Gote's attackers one at a time.","83":"Px6e. The pawn on 6f takes the knight on 6e. Gote loses another attacker, and Sente's pieces in hand grow.","84":"S*7h. Gote drops a silver that aims at the rook on 6i. Sente will lose the rook, but in the meantime it hurries its own attack.","85":"P*9b. Now Sente's counterattack begins. A pawn dropped on 9b, near Gote's king, pulls the lance forward. Moving the pieces around the king creates gaps.","86":"Lx9b. Gote takes the pawn with the lance. But now the lance stands on 9b, and it becomes a target for the next knight.","87":"N*8d. Sente drops a knight that attacks both the lance on 9b and the gold on 7b. Hitting two things at once is just what a knight does best.","88":"B-5a. Gote pulls the bishop back so that it aims along the diagonal at the knight on 8d. Gote wants to take the knight and stop the attack.","89":"N*7d, check. Sente drops its other knight, and it checks Gote's king. A knight can check by jumping over other pieces, so it is hard to defend against.","90":"K-8c. Gote's king steps up. Moving forward also brings it near Sente's knights.","91":"Nx9b+. The knight on 8d takes the lance and promotes, becoming a piece that moves like a gold. Sente has broken into the area near Gote's king.","92":"Kx9b. Gote takes the promoted knight with the king. Gote's king now stands on the edge at 9b.","93":"Bx7g. Sente's bishop takes the pawn on 7g. The bishop has not moved all game, but now it starts working. It will play a big part later.","94":"Sx6i+. Gote's silver takes Sente's rook and promotes. Losing a rook is a big loss, but Sente judges that its attack on the king is faster.","95":"Gx6i. Sente takes back the promoted silver with the gold. Sente's defending gold is still standing firm.","97":"P*9c, check. A pawn is cheap, but dropped right in front of a king it becomes very strong.","98":"K-8a. Gote's king retreats to 8a. Its escape routes are getting fewer.","99":"Bx8f. The bishop takes the pawn on 8f. Along its diagonal it now sees deep into Gote's camp. It is preparing the next big move.","101":"G*9b, check. The gold on 9b and the pawn on 9c work together to drive Gote's king off the edge.","103":"B-5c+. The bishop on 8f flies all the way to 5c and promotes into a horse. A horse moves like a bishop plus one step in any direction, a very strong piece. Sente's big piece has broken in close to Gote's king.","104":"K-6a. Gote's king runs on to file 6. It is being pushed from the edge toward the middle of the board.","105":"L*6d. Sente drops a lance. A lance moves straight forward as far as it likes, so it watches the whole of file 6.","106":"P*6b. Gote drops a pawn to block the lance's path.","107":"+Bx4c. The horse takes the silver on 4c. Gote loses another defender.","108":"N*5b. Gote drops a knight to strengthen its defence. The position is hard, but Gote keeps fighting.","109":"K-8g. Here Sente moves its own king up to 8g once. It seems to be giving the king more room before Gote strikes back.","110":"R*8i, check. Gote strikes back, dropping a rook to give check. Gote is trying to catch Sente's king too.","112":"P-7g+, check. The pawn promotes into a tokin and gives check. A tokin moves like a gold, yet if it is captured the opponent only gets a pawn.","114":"Rx6i+. The rook takes the gold and becomes a dragon. Sente's king has lost its defenders, and the game becomes a race: who catches the other king first?","115":"S*5c. Sente drops a silver to tighten the net around Gote's king. Pressing the enemy king even when your own king is in danger is the heart of such a race.","116":"S*6f, check. Gote drops a silver and checks. Sente's king must run.","117":"K-8f. The king escapes to 8f. Sente's king has plenty of room upward.","119":"K-9e. The king goes on to 9e at the edge. Gote's pieces can hardly reach it here. Sente's king is safe for now, so Sente can focus on attacking.","120":"P-6c. Gote pushes a pawn to defend. But Gote's defence is no longer enough.","121":"S*6b. Here Sente begins the finish. It drops a silver right into Gote's camp to take the pieces guarding the king.","122":"Bx6b. Gote takes the silver with the bishop.","123":"Sx6b+. The silver on 5c takes the bishop and promotes. Gote loses a big piece.","125":"Nx6b+. The knight on 7d takes the gold and promotes as well. Trading piece for piece, Sente strips Gote's defence bare.","126":"Kx6b. Gote takes with the king. But there are almost no defenders left around Gote's king.","127":"B*5c, check. Sente drops the bishop it just captured. Using captured pieces right away is what makes shogi so exciting.","129":"G*8d, check. The gold closes the king's escape from above.","131":"P*7c, check. Gote's king has no choice but to go back deeper.","133":"Lx6c+. The lance takes the pawn on 6c and promotes. Seeing that a piece coming to 6b next would leave the king no escape, Sansa admitted defeat here. Sōkei wins in 133 moves. The highlights were the 'rook chase' of the opening, the middle-game tug of war with pawns and knights, and the ending, where Sente gave up its rook to race at the king. It shows how the ideas of a master 400 years ago still connect to the shogi of today."}},"hu1960":{"t":"Hu Ronghua, aged 15, beats Yang Guanlin","w":"Yang Guanlin","b":"Hu Ronghua","ev":"5th National Xiangqi Individual Championship, round 3","d":"28 October 1960","pl":"Beijing","lead":"Yang Guanlin had been national champion three times. Facing him with Black was Hu Ronghua, a 15-year-old playing his first national tournament. Hu gave up a cannon early to seize the initiative, then won a long endgame. Hu went on to win the tournament and then ten national titles in a row, so this game is remembered as the moment the crown passed from the old champion to the new.","c":{"0":"This is round 3 of the 5th National Xiangqi Individual Championship, held in Beijing in 1960. Red is Yang Guanlin. He won the first championship in 1956 and three of the four tournaments so far — the strongest player of the time. Black is Hu Ronghua from Shanghai, only 15 years old and playing his first national tournament. Everyone expected Red to win, but this game would change xiangqi history.","1":"Yang moves the right cannon to the middle. The Central Cannon aims straight at Black’s general, and it was the opening Yang had studied most deeply.","2":"Hu jumps a horse out to guard the middle soldier. Bringing the horse out early prepares both defence and attack.","3":"Red also jumps a horse out, guarding the middle soldier while bringing pieces forward. Both sides make the centre safe first.","4":"Black pushes the soldier on file 7 early. It holds back Red’s horse and opens a path for Black’s own horse. In this position most players bring out a chariot first.","5":"Red moves the right chariot one step sideways onto an open file.","6":"Black also moves a chariot sideways. Both sides want their strongest piece, the chariot, working early.","7":"Here Red pushes a soldier. Many players would send the chariot across the river, but Yang is steering toward the cannon set-up he liked best.","8":"Black’s cannon advances to the river and stands right in front of Red’s right chariot. This plan, called “left cannon blocks the chariot”, shuts the chariot in at home. It was a bold idea to stop Yang from reaching his favourite set-up.","9":"Red brings out the other horse, developing the left side and preparing to strike back.","10":"Black lifts an elephant to strengthen the defence. Elephants cannot cross the river; their job is to guard the general’s house.","11":"Red’s cannon dives deep into Black’s camp, takes a horse and gives check! It trades a cannon for a horse. Trading a cannon for a horse early often costs time, but here it is said to be a fair move.","12":"Black recaptures the cannon with the chariot. Material is level again, and Black’s chariot has come out naturally.","13":"Red brings out the left chariot too. Red would like to push the black cannons away and get the chariots moving.","14":"Black pushes the other cannon to the river as well, blocking Red’s left chariot. Now both of Red’s chariots have a cannon sitting in front of them. Red is getting cramped.","15":"Red moves the horse toward the river. Red has two threats in mind: taking the middle soldier with the horse, or pushing a soldier to attack Black’s cannon.","16":"Black lifts an advisor to guard the centre. This stops the horse from taking the middle soldier with check.","17":"Red pushes the soldier. That opens the path the soldier was blocking, so now Red’s horse can capture Black’s cannon. The cannon is under attack.","18":"This is the most famous moment of the game. Black does not save the cannon. Instead the chariot moves up to attack Red’s horse. Hu gives up a cannon to keep the attacking shape. His opponent was a three-time champion and he was only 15, yet he chose energy over material — and the spectators were amazed.","19":"Red’s horse takes Black’s cannon. Black is a piece down. In return, Black’s chariot is now pressing on Red’s chariot and horse from the side.","20":"Black’s other cannon takes the middle soldier — check! Black’s counterattack begins. Red has to lift an advisor to protect the general.","21":"Red lifts an advisor to block the check. But Black’s cannon is still sitting deep inside Red’s camp.","22":"Black’s chariot takes Red’s chariot. When the cannon moved away a moment ago, the chariot’s path opened up.","23":"Red takes back with the horse — a chariot for a chariot. Black is still a piece down, but Black’s pieces are now working better.","24":"Black’s horse advances to the river. One idea is to go after Red’s horse next.","26":"Black’s cannon slides across and stops right beside Red’s horse. A xiangqi horse cannot move in a direction if a piece stands right next to it on that side (the horse’s “leg”). With its leg blocked by the cannon, Red’s horse can hardly move.","28":"Black pushes the middle soldier. Later this soldier will become an important attacker in Red’s camp.","30":"Black’s horse takes a soldier and slips into Red’s camp. Little by little, Black wins back material.","32":"Black’s horse comes back to the middle. It avoids being traded for Red’s horse and takes a fine central point.","34":"Black’s cannon steps back one point. It is getting ready to slide across and attack Red’s horse.","36":"Black’s cannon slides over and, using Black’s own chariot as a screen, attacks Red’s horse. A cannon captures by jumping over exactly one piece.","38":"Black’s horse jumps out and attacks Red’s chariot. By moving away, it also takes away the screen Red’s cannon needed to attack Black’s chariot.","40":"Red’s cannon gave check, so Black drops the advisor back to block. Red is fighting back hard.","42":"Black’s cannon takes Red’s horse! Black wins back the cannon given up in the opening. Material is level, Black has an extra soldier, and all three of Black’s attacking pieces stand across the river. Black is now clearly better.","44":"Black pushes a soldier forward and offers it for free. If Red takes it, a hidden plan becomes possible — a move that looks far ahead.","45":"Red takes the soldier with an elephant. Now Red must watch out for Black’s plan.","46":"Black offers the horse as well. If Red’s horse takes it, Black’s cannon can give check while uncovering an attack on Red’s chariot.","47":"Red does not take the horse and pulls its own horse back instead. Yang, an old champion, does not fall into traps easily.","48":"Black’s chariot slides sideways and frees itself from the pin. Now the chariot can move freely again, for attack or defence.","50":"Black’s cannon moves to the middle, staring straight at Red’s general — a very strong post.","52":"Black’s horse jumps forward again. Cannon and horse are working together in the centre.","56":"Black’s horse goes all the way to the edge and eyes the side of Red’s general. A horse on the edge has fewer moves, but here it has the important job of threatening the general’s shelter.","58":"Black’s middle soldier moves further forward, pressing on Red’s camp. Once a soldier crosses the river it can also move sideways, so it becomes much stronger.","60":"The cannons are traded off. Red’s cannon took Black’s cannon, and Black’s soldier took Red’s cannon back. Black’s soldier is now close to Red’s camp.","62":"Black’s chariot takes one of Red’s elephants. Red’s defence loses another piece.","64":"Black’s horse gives check! Red blocks the horse’s leg with the chariot. In xiangqi you can stop a horse’s check by putting a piece right next to the horse.","66":"Black’s horse steps back for now. It keeps giving checks while looking for its best square.","70":"Black’s horse checks again. Red’s chariot cannot leave the general’s side, so it cannot join the attack.","72":"Black’s horse drops back to the edge. It keeps Red’s chariot tied down while Black plans the next step.","76":"Black’s chariot takes Red’s other elephant too. Red’s general is becoming very lonely.","82":"Black’s chariot takes Red’s horse. Red’s hard-working horse is traded off here.","83":"Red takes back Black’s horse with the chariot. With the horses traded, the board is much clearer. Now it is a fight of chariot and soldiers on both sides.","90":"Black’s soldier takes one of Red’s advisors! A soldier is a small piece, but deep in the enemy camp it is strong enough to trade itself for an advisor.","91":"Red takes the soldier back with the other advisor. A soldier for an advisor — Red’s general is even less protected.","92":"Black’s chariot takes a Red soldier. In the endgame, the number of soldiers left decides who wins.","98":"Black’s chariot takes another soldier. Red has only a few soldiers left. Step by step, Black is heading for an easier winning position.","99":"Red’s chariot takes a Black elephant in return. The two chariots are trading blows, taking one piece each.","104":"Black starts pushing the soldier on the edge. Bringing this soldier deep into Red’s camp is Black’s road to victory.","112":"Black’s chariot goes behind the soldier to protect it. A chariot and a soldier together make a very strong team.","115":"Red’s chariot takes one of Black’s soldiers. Black has one soldier left. Red has not given up yet.","123":"Red pushes the soldier forward. But it is said that pulling the chariot back to guard the general was better here. This move became the turning point of the game.","124":"Black’s soldier steps sideways toward Red’s general. A soldier moves only one step at a time, but the closer it gets to the general, the more dangerous it becomes.","126":"Black’s chariot drops back and attacks Red’s soldier. Black must keep that soldier in check, or Red could fight back with it.","134":"Red’s soldier has reached the far end of the board, but Black’s chariot has been chasing it all the way. A soldier that reaches the far end can no longer go forward, only sideways.","136":"Black’s chariot takes Red’s last soldier. Black has a chariot, a soldier and two advisors; Red has a chariot and one advisor. With an extra soldier, Black’s win is now clearly in sight.","140":"Black’s chariot gives check! Red’s general can only step back.","144":"Black’s soldier advances again and is now right next to Red’s general. With soldier and chariot working together, Red’s general is no longer safe.","148":"Black’s general also steps sideways. In the endgame the general is a fighting piece too, controlling where the enemy pieces can go.","150":"Black’s chariot drops back, ready for both defence and attack. It is getting the chariot ready for the best square for the final blow.","152":"Black’s chariot enters file 4, and Red can no longer hold. The game is decided, and 15-year-old Hu Ronghua has won. A bold cannon sacrifice in the opening, teamwork of horse and cannon in the middlegame, and precise play through a long endgame — this game had all three. Hu won the tournament and went on to be national champion ten times in a row, from 1960 to 1979."}},"soei1790":{"t":"Demon Sōei beats the Meijin in an even game","w":"Ōhashi Sōei (VI)","b":"Ōhashi Sōkei (IX)","ev":"Oshiro Shogi (the yearly game at Edo Castle)","d":"1790","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"Ōhashi Sōei, nicknamed 'Demon Sōei', played this even game against the reigning Meijin, Ōhashi Sōkei IX, at the castle games. That year the two alternated even games and lance-handicap games, and this even game is remembered as a rare masterpiece. Sōei won in 121 moves.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. The castle games (Oshiro Shogi) were a great yearly event, where the shogi houses played before the Shōgun. Sente, Ōhashi Sōei, was so strong that people called him 'Demon Sōei'; he later became the ninth Meijin. Gote, Ōhashi Sōkei IX, had become Meijin only the year before. So this is the Meijin against the next Meijin. Sōei spread the idea of building a solid defence first and attacking after, close to how shogi is played today, and he is even called the father of modern shogi.","1":"P-7f. The first move opens the bishop's diagonal. From here Sōei builds a plan to attack with his right-side pieces.","3":"P-2f. Sente pushes the pawn in front of the rook. Pushing the rook's pawn early to press the opponent was one of the ideas Sōei valued.","4":"P-4d. Gote pushes a pawn that closes its bishop's diagonal. It avoids a bishop trade and prepares for a slow game.","5":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver so it can be used both to attack and to defend.","7":"P-5f. Sente pushes the centre pawn so that silvers and golds can come forward more easily.","9":"P-3f. Sente pushes the file-3 pawn too, opening a road for the knight to jump. It prepares N-3g.","11":"P-2e. Sente pushes the rook's pawn further. Now Gote must guard file 2 with the bishop or a silver.","12":"B-3c. Gote guards file 2 with the bishop. Without it, Sente's rook would break through on file 2 after P-2d.","15":"G-5h. Sente brings a gold closer to the king and starts to build its defence.","17":"N-3g. The knight jumps up and Sente's attacking shape is ready. From here the knight can leap to 4e or 2e to reach Gote's camp.","20":"R-5b. Gote moves its rook to the centre file 5. Today this is called the Central Rook. The aim is to shake Sente's camp from the middle.","22":"P-5e. Gote at once pushes its file-5 pawn into Sente's. As Central Rook players like to, it starts the fight in the middle.","23":"Px5e. Sente takes the pawn. If not, Gote's pawn would take the pawn on 5f.","24":"S-6d. Gote brings a silver forward, aiming to win back the pawn on 5e.","25":"P-5d. Sente pushes the captured pawn on even further. Placing it right in front of Gote's rook makes Gote take it, which moves Gote's pieces out of their best squares.","26":"Rx5d. Gote takes the pawn with the rook. Gote's rook has come out to 5d and is now strong in the centre.","27":"B-7i. Sente pulls the bishop back. Stepping back once lets it work later along a different diagonal. This bishop will later come out to 2d.","28":"K-4a. Gote moves its king to 4a. With a Central Rook, the king usually hides on the right side.","29":"P*5f. Sente drops a pawn to stop Gote's rook from advancing along file 5.","30":"P*5e. Gote drops a pawn too and clashes again on file 5. The tug of war in the centre goes on.","32":"Px5f. Gote's pawn takes Sente's pawn and bites into Sente's camp.","34":"S-5e. Gote brings the silver out to take control of file 5.","35":"Gx5e. Sente takes the silver with the gold. A gold and a silver are about to be traded.","36":"Rx5e. Gote takes back the gold with the rook. After this gold-for-silver trade, the fight in the centre calms down. Sente now has a silver in hand, and Gote has a gold.","37":"P*5f. Sente drops a pawn to block file 5, so Gote's rook cannot come any further.","38":"R-5b. Gote pulls its rook back. The fight in the centre is over for now.","39":"G-5h. Sente lifts its other gold and makes the area around the king firmer.","41":"K-6i. Sente's king starts moving left, away from the centre where the fighting happened.","43":"K-7h. The king reaches 7h. From here Sente will build a castle with golds and silvers. Sōei was a player who believed in defending first and attacking second.","44":"R-8b. Gote returns its rook to file 8. Now that the centre is quiet, it plans to attack on file 8 instead.","45":"S*7g. Sente drops the silver from its hand in front of the king. It prepares in advance for Gote's attack on file 8.","47":"P-1f. Sente pushes the edge pawn. An advanced edge pawn helps later, whether for an edge attack or as an escape route for the king.","49":"P-1e. The edge pawn goes one step further. Sente is preparing carefully.","51":"P-2d. Here Sente starts its attack, pushing the rook's pawn it has been advancing all along. Sente's offensive begins.","53":"Bx2d. The bishop that retreated to 7i flies all the way along the diagonal to 2d. Now we can see why Sente pulled the bishop back on move 27.","54":"Bx2d. Gote takes the bishop with its own bishop. The bishops are traded.","55":"Rx2d. Sente takes back with the rook, which now stands on 2d glaring at Gote's camp. Each side now has a bishop in hand.","56":"P*2c. Gote drops a pawn to block the rook's path.","57":"R-2f. Sente pulls the rook back. It doesn't push too far, and brings the rook back to safety.","59":"P-4f. Sente pushes the file-4 pawn to prepare the next attack. After file 2, the plan is to attack on files 3 and 4.","60":"R-8d. Gote lifts its rook so it can also move sideways.","61":"P-3e. Sente pushes the file-3 pawn, getting ready to clash with Gote's pawn on 3d.","63":"P-4e. Sente pushes its file-4 pawn into Gote's. Sente shakes Gote on several files at once: 3, 4 and 5.","65":"P-5e. Sente pushes the file-5 pawn forward too, driving a wedge into the middle of Gote's defence.","66":"P*5c. Gote drops a pawn to guard file 5.","67":"Px3d. Sente's pawn takes the pawn on 3d. Later this pawn will reach 3c and promote, pressing Gote's king.","68":"P-8f. Gote strikes back, pushing its file-8 pawn to start a fire near Sente's king.","69":"Sx8f. Sente takes with the silver. The silver from 7g steps forward and defends while keeping the king safe.","70":"B*4d. Gote drops the bishop from its hand. Along its diagonals it watches Sente's camp, ready to defend and attack.","71":"R-2i. Sente pulls the rook all the way back. It escapes the bishop's line and still watches file 2.","72":"P-6e. Gote pushes the file-6 pawn to shake the area near Sente's king.","74":"P*3f. Gote drops a pawn that attacks the knight on 3g.","75":"B*6f. Sente drops its bishop from hand. Along the diagonal it attacks the rook on 8d. It defends and attacks a big piece at the same time: two birds with one stone.","76":"R-8a. Gote runs the rook away. With just one bishop move, Sente has pushed Gote's rook all the way back to the bottom.","77":"Nx4e. The knight jumps and takes the pawn on 4e. Next it aims to jump into 3c or 5c.","78":"G*4f. Gote drops a gold that presses on the knight from behind.","79":"P-3c+. The pawn from 3d advances to 3c and promotes into a tokin. A Sente piece has broken in near Gote's king.","80":"Nx3c. Gote takes the tokin with its knight.","81":"Nx3c+. Sente's knight on 4e takes that knight and promotes. Trading piece for piece, Sente thins out the defenders around Gote's king.","82":"Bx3c. Gote takes back the promoted knight with the bishop.","83":"S-5g. Sente moves the silver up to 5g to clash with Gote's gold on 4f, to drive away the gold that came so close.","85":"Gx5g. Silver and gold have been traded. Sente has neatly cleared away the Gote piece that came into its camp.","86":"P-7e. Gote pushes its file-7 pawn, aiming at the head of Sente's king.","87":"N*4e. Sente drops a knight again. This knight attacks the bishop on 3c.","88":"B-2d. Gote moves the bishop away to 2d.","89":"P*3c. Sente drops a pawn to drive a wedge near Gote's king.","90":"G-2b. Gote steps the gold back to defend.","91":"P-5d. Sente pushes the file-5 pawn further, luring Gote's silver to 5d.","92":"Sx5d. Gote takes the pawn with the silver. But this leaves 4c empty, which links to Sente's next moves.","93":"Rx2d. This is the turning point. Sente takes Gote's bishop with the rook. Even if the rook is lost, Sente judges it can rush at Gote's king in the meantime.","94":"Px2d. Gote takes the rook with a pawn. Sente has given up its rook but won a bishop, an attacking piece, for its hand.","95":"B*3b, check. Sente drops the bishop. Following Rx2d, this is the real start of Sente's attack.","96":"K-5a. Gote's king escapes to 5a.","97":"Bx5d+. The bishop takes the silver on 5d and promotes into a horse. Since 4c was empty, the bishop could reach it straight along the diagonal. The empty square left by Sx5d on move 92 pays off here.","98":"Px5d. Gote takes the horse with a pawn. Sente has given away all its big pieces, but Gote's defending silver is gone too.","99":"P*5c. Sente drops a pawn right in front of Gote's king as a foothold. Next it aims to promote on 5b.","100":"R*4h. Gote strikes back, dropping the rook it won into Sente's camp. The game becomes a race: whose attack is faster?","101":"S-5h. Sente pulls the silver back to defend, blocking the rook from sliding sideways toward the king.","102":"B*5f, check. Gote drops a bishop. From 5f it reaches the king on 7h diagonally through 6g.","103":"K-8h. Sente steps the king down, out of the bishop's line. With this defensive move it makes sure it won't lose the race.","104":"Bx4e. Gote's bishop takes the knight.","105":"Px5b+. The pawn promotes into a tokin and takes Gote's gold. A Sente piece is now right beside Gote's king.","106":"Kx5b. Gote takes the tokin with the king.","107":"P*5c, check. Pawns can be dropped again and again, so Sente can keep checking like this (but you may not have two of your own unpromoted pawns on the same file).","108":"K-6b. Gote's king escapes to 6b.","109":"S*6d. Sente drops a silver to close the king's escape routes.","110":"S*7d. Gote drops a silver too, to defend.","111":"P-5b+. Sente makes a tokin and attacks the king from behind as well.","112":"K-7b. Gote's king goes to 7b. Step by step, it is being chased toward the edge.","113":"Sx7c+. The silver takes the knight on 7c and promotes. Another of Gote's defenders is gone.","114":"Kx7c. Gote takes the promoted silver with the king.","115":"G*6d, check. From here Sente uses the pieces in its hand to hunt down Gote's king.","116":"K-8c. Gote's king goes to 8c.","117":"Gx7d, check. The gold takes the silver on 7d with check, stripping away the defenders around Gote's king.","118":"Kx7d. Gote takes the gold with the king. The king has been dragged out toward the middle of the board.","119":"Sx7e, check. The silver on 8f takes a pawn with check. The silver that stepped up to defend on move 69 joins the attack at the end.","120":"K-6c. Gote's king escapes to 6c.","121":"S-6d, check. The silver on 7e moves to 6d with check. Sente still has a gold, a silver and a knight in hand, and seeing that the king could not escape, Sōkei admitted defeat here. Sōei wins in 121 moves. After the fight in the centre, Sente first built a firm castle, then widened the attack across files 2, 3 and 4, and finally gave up the rook to rush the king. It is a masterpiece between Meijin that shows Sōei's shogi of 'defend first, then attack'."}},"soho1856":{"t":"Sōho the Saint and his far-seeing bishop","w":"Amano Sōho","b":"Itō Sōin (VIII)","ev":"Oshiro Shogi (the yearly game at Edo Castle)","d":"1856","pl":"Japan (Edo)","lead":"Amano Sōho, called the 'Saint of Shogi', played this castle game against Itō Sōin VIII, a future Meijin, and it became Sōho's last castle game. On move 33 he dropped a bishop into the corner of his own camp, B*1h, a move remembered in shogi history as a masterstroke. Sōho wins in 111 moves.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. Amano Sōho played near the end of the Edo period, and he was so strong that people called him 'a true 13-dan' and the 'Saint of Shogi'. But he was not born into one of the shogi houses, so he could never become Meijin. Gote, Itō Sōin VIII, was Sōho's junior in the same school and later became Meijin. This castle game of 1856 was the last castle game Sōho ever played. Sōho is said to have been a master of the bishop, so keep an eye on the bishops in this game.","1":"P-7f. The first move opens the bishop's diagonal. Sōho's bishop will be the star of this game.","3":"P-2f. Sente pushes the pawn in front of the rook. Sōho liked fast openings, pushing the rook's pawn early and getting his pieces moving.","4":"P-5d. Gote pushes the centre pawn. We can see a plan coming: the rook will swing to file 5 for a Central Rook.","7":"S-4h. Sente lifts the right silver to prepare for both attack and defence.","11":"P-2e. Sente pushes the rook's pawn further. Next it plans to clash on 2d.","12":"S-6d. Gote brings a silver forward. The idea is to advance it toward 5e and attack in the centre.","13":"P-2d. Sente pushes the pawn into Gote's to trade off the rook's pawn. It is a natural way to start a fight, still common today.","15":"Rx2d. The rook takes the pawn. Sente gains a pawn in hand and the rook comes forward. Trading the rook's pawn gives you an extra pawn in hand and makes the rook easier to use.","16":"R-5b. Gote moves its rook to file 5. With a Central Rook it plans to strike back in the middle.","17":"K-6i. Sente moves the king to the left, away from the centre where the Central Rook will attack.","19":"R-2h. The rook goes back home. Having won a pawn, Sente calmly pulls the rook back.","20":"P*2c. Gote drops a pawn to close the gap on file 2.","21":"Bx2b+. Here Sente trades bishops. After a bishop trade, both sides hold a bishop in hand that can be dropped anywhere. From now on, Sōho shows his skill in where he drops his bishop.","22":"Sx2b. Gote takes back with the silver. Each side now has a bishop in hand.","24":"P-5e. Gote pushes its centre pawn into Sente's. It is a typical Central Rook attack.","26":"Sx5e. Gote's silver comes all the way to 5e. Gote's pieces are getting strong in the centre.","27":"P*5d. Sente drops a pawn deep into Gote's camp. The pawn on 5d blocks the front of Gote's rook and gets in the way of Gote's pieces.","29":"B*5c. Sente drops its bishop into Gote's camp. The idea is to let it run wild inside Gote's position.","30":"B*4d. Gote drops its bishop too, clashing with Sente's. It is a defensive move to get rid of Sente's bishop.","31":"Bx4d+. Sente's bishop takes Gote's bishop and promotes.","32":"Sx4d. Gote takes the horse back with the silver. The bishops have been traded again, and Sente once more has a bishop in hand.","33":"B*1h. This is the famous moment. Sōho drops his bishop in the corner of his own camp, on 1h. Along its diagonal it looks all the way across the board into Gote's camp. For now it supports the pawn on 5d from behind, and later, when the diagonal opens, it will do great work: a 'far-seeing bishop'. It is called one of the masterstrokes of shogi history, though some later Meijin think it may have been a clever way out of a difficult spot.","34":"G-4b. Gote brings a gold over to firm up the area around the king.","35":"S-5g. Sente lifts a silver to prepare for the fight in the centre.","37":"S-5f. The silver moves further forward, aiming at the centre squares 5e and 4e.","39":"R-5h. Sente moves the rook to the centre. Sente too gathers strength on file 5, in a Central Rook shape.","41":"P-6f. Sente pushes the file-6 pawn to make a path for its silver and bishop.","43":"K-7i. The king goes deeper to the left. Even Sōho puts the king in order before attacking.","45":"G-5i. Sente brings a gold near the king to strengthen its defence.","46":"P-8e. Gote pushes its file-8 pawn, preparing to attack from near Sente's king.","47":"B-2g. The bishop on 1h steps up one square. Shifting its line slightly, it now eyes 4e and 3f.","49":"S-4e. The silver advances to 4e and presses on Gote's camp. The bishop on 2g supports this silver from behind.","50":"P*5e. Gote drops a pawn to block the centre.","52":"P-8f. Gote starts a fight on file 8, pushing a pawn into the pawn near Sente's king.","54":"P-7e. Gote pushes the file-7 pawn into Sente's too. It is trying to break several of the pawns in front of Sente's king.","56":"Sx4e. Gote's silver takes Sente's silver on 4e.","57":"Bx4e. The bishop on 2g takes back the silver on 4e. The bishop's diagonal comes in handy here.","58":"S-4d. Gote lifts a silver to chase the bishop on 4e.","59":"B-1h. The bishop goes back to the corner at 1h again. Chased by the silver, it returns to its far-seeing spot. Watch how this bishop works at the end.","60":"S*5f. Gote drops a silver and attacks in the centre.","61":"Sx5f. Sente takes with its silver.","62":"Px5f. Gote takes back with a pawn, which now reaches 5f.","63":"Rx5f. Sente takes the pawn with the rook. The rook works in the centre.","64":"S*5e. Gote drops another silver to chase Sente's rook.","65":"R-5h. The rook steps back to where it was.","66":"Sx6f. Gote's silver takes the pawn on 6f and comes closer to Sente's king. Gote's attack goes on.","67":"P*6g. Sente drops a pawn so the silver cannot come in any further.","68":"P*7g. Gote drops a pawn on 7g, near Sente's king. It wants to move Sente's pieces around and make a gap.","69":"Nx7g. Sente takes the pawn with the knight. Later this knight will jump to 6e and join the attack.","70":"Sx7e. Gote's silver takes the pawn on 7e and closes in on the head of Sente's king.","71":"G-6h. Sente brings a gold over to guard the king.","72":"P*7f. Gote drops another pawn, aiming at the knight. Gote's attack is getting sharp.","73":"N-6e. Here Sente strikes back. The knight jumps to 6e and aims at 5c in Gote's camp.","74":"S-6d. Gote pulls the silver back to aim at the knight on 6e.","75":"S*5c. Sente drops a silver deep into Gote's camp. The pawn on 5d and the knight on 6e both support it. The pawn Sente dropped on 5d long ago is useful here.","76":"Gx5c. Gote takes the silver with the gold.","77":"Px5c+. The pawn on 5d takes the gold and promotes into a tokin.","78":"Sx5c. Gote takes the tokin with the silver.","79":"Nx5c+. The knight on 6e takes that silver and promotes. The captures go on, and Gote's camp grows emptier and emptier.","80":"Sx5c. Gote takes the promoted knight with the silver. But now Sente's pawn on 5d is gone, so the diagonal of the bishop on 1h is open all the way to 6c.","81":"Bx6c+. At last the bishop on 1h moves. It flies down the diagonal, takes the pawn on 6c and becomes a horse. The 'far-seeing bishop' dropped on move 33 does its big job here, 48 moves later. It is a true bishop's move, running from one edge of the board to the other.","82":"S*7g. Gote drops a silver to press on Sente's king. Gote keeps attacking too.","83":"P*2d. Sente drops a pawn on file 2 to shake the area near Gote's king.","84":"Px2d. Gote takes it with a pawn.","85":"P*2e. Sente drops another pawn. It aims at Gote's pawn on 2d and tries to pry open file 2.","86":"B*3c. Gote drops a bishop to strengthen its defence.","87":"P*5e. Sente drops a pawn to hold back the movements of Gote's silver and bishop.","88":"Sx7h+. Gote's silver takes Sente's gold and promotes.","89":"Gx7h. Sente takes back with the gold.","90":"G*7g. Gote drops a gold that presses on Sente's king. The fighting near Sente's king is fierce too.","91":"S*4e. Sente drops a silver. This silver aims at 3d, near Gote's king.","92":"R-6a. Gote moves its rook to file 6 to attack Sente's horse.","93":"+B-7d. The horse moves away to 7d. A horse is strong: it helps in its own camp and in the enemy's.","94":"Px2e. Gote's pawn takes the pawn on 2e.","95":"Sx3d. The silver takes the pawn on 3d. Sente's silver is right in front of Gote's king.","96":"B-2d. Gote moves its bishop to 2d to prepare for Sente's attack.","97":"S*4f. Sente drops another silver. It cuts the diagonal of the bishop on 2d and limits what that bishop can do.","98":"G*3c. Gote drops a gold to defend.","99":"Sx3c+. The silver on 3d takes the gold and promotes.","100":"Kx3c. Gote takes the promoted silver with the king. Gote's king has come out to 3c, where there are few defenders.","101":"S*3e. Sente drops another silver. It attacks the bishop on 2d and also blocks the king's escape.","102":"N*8g, check. Gote drops a knight to check. It is a last stand.","103":"Gx8g. Sente takes the knight with the gold.","104":"Gx8g. Gote takes back with the gold. But Sente's king is still not caught.","105":"Sx2d, check. The silver takes the bishop with check. From here Sente hunts Gote's king all at once.","106":"Kx2d. Gote takes the silver with the king.","107":"S-3e, check. The silver on 4f moves to 3e with check, pulling Gote's king out of its camp.","108":"Kx3e. Gote's king takes the silver and comes out to 3e. When a king comes out toward the middle with no defenders, it is in great danger.","109":"B*4f, check. The bishop again, right to the end. Sente drops the bishop from its hand with check. In Sōho's game, the bishops played a starring role from beginning to end.","110":"K-4d. Gote's king escapes to 4d.","111":"G*3d, check. Seeing that the king could no longer escape, Sōin admitted defeat here. Sōho wins in 111 moves. B*1h on move 33 did great work 48 moves later with Bx6c+, and the game was decided by a bishop check too. It is a masterpiece that shows Sōho's way of placing a piece while looking far into the future."}},"sakata1913":{"t":"Sakata Sankichi beats Sekine with the Side Rook","w":"Sakata Sankichi","b":"Sekine Kinjirō","ev":"A game sponsored by the Osaka Asahi newspaper","d":"14 July 1913","pl":"Japan (Osaka)","lead":"Sakata Sankichi of Osaka and Sekine Kinjirō, who later became the 13th Meijin, were rivals all their lives. This is an even game they played in Osaka in the summer of 1913. Sakata attacked with the 'Side Rook', moving his rook to file 3, and won a long battle of 143 moves.","c":{"0":"I'm Musowl. Sakata Sankichi was born in Sakai near Osaka, learned shogi almost entirely by himself, and became the strongest player in western Japan. Sekine Kinjirō was the top player in Tokyo and later became the 13th Meijin. They had played each other many times since they were young, more than 30 games in all: a famous rivalry. In April of this year, Sakata went to Tokyo for the first time and played Sekine. The words 'my silver is crying' are said to come from that game, which was a lance-handicap game. This July game is an even game against Sekine, who had come to Osaka.","1":"P-7f. The first move opens the bishop's diagonal. From here Sakata builds a plan full of his own ideas.","3":"P-5f. Sente pushes the centre pawn, preparing to bring a silver up to 5g.","5":"P-6f. Sente pushes the file-6 pawn too and closes the bishop's diagonal. It avoids a bishop trade and builds its position slowly.","9":"S-6g. A silver moves up in front of the king and starts a defensive wall.","11":"B-7g. The bishop steps up behind the pawn on 6f. Using the bishop to defend gives a shape close to the Fortress castle.","13":"S-4h. Sente lifts the other silver too, ready to use it in the attack.","16":"K-4b. Gote, Sekine, moves his king toward the right side.","17":"G-7h. A gold comes toward the king to make the defence firmer.","19":"P-3f. Sente pushes the file-3 pawn. This is where Sakata's plan begins.","21":"R-3h. Sente moves the rook to file 3. This is the 'Side Rook'. Instead of file 2, the rook stands on the file next to it, file 3, to attack there. Sakata often used unusual plans like this.","23":"K-6i. Sente's king goes left, away from file 3, where the attack will be.","26":"K-3b. Gote's king goes to 3b. But that is right next to file 3, which Sente's rook is aiming at.","27":"P-3e. The Side Rook attack begins. Sente pushes the file-3 pawn into Gote's and tries to break through on file 3 with the rook.","28":"Px3e. Gote takes with the pawn.","29":"Rx3e. The rook takes the pawn. Sente's rook comes out to 3e and glares at the area near Gote's king. Sente has also won a pawn.","30":"B-3a. Gote pulls the bishop back to prepare to defend file 3.","31":"R-3h. Sente pulls the rook back. Having won a pawn, it calmly brings the rook home.","32":"P-8f. Gote strikes back on file 8.","34":"Bx8f. The bishop that went back to 3a comes all the way along the diagonal to 8f and takes the pawn. A clever use of the bishop's long line.","35":"Bx8f. Sente takes the bishop with its bishop.","36":"Rx8f. Gote takes back with the rook. The bishops have been traded.","37":"P*8g. Sente drops a pawn to chase Gote's rook.","38":"R-8b. Gote's rook goes back home. Each side now has a bishop in hand.","39":"S-4f. Sente brings a silver forward to prepare for fights in the centre and on file 4.","43":"K-7i. Sente tucks the king in deeper and puts its defence in order.","47":"K-8h. The king settles on 8h, with golds and silvers gathered around it.","51":"P-5e. Sente pushes the centre pawn into Gote's. From here the real fighting starts.","52":"Px5e. Gote takes with the pawn.","53":"B*6c. Sente drops its bishop into Gote's camp. The idea is to let it run wild inside Gote's position.","55":"R-5h. Sente swings the rook to file 5 to join the fight in the centre.","56":"B*9b. Gote drops its bishop too. Along its diagonal it stares at Sente's camp.","57":"B-3f+. The bishop on 6c comes back along the diagonal and promotes as it leaves the enemy camp, becoming a horse on 3f. Back in its own camp, it can both defend and attack. A horse in your own camp is a very reliable defender.","58":"P-4e. Gote pushes a pawn to chase the silver on 4f.","59":"S-5g. The silver steps back to 5g.","60":"Px7f. Gote's file-7 pawn takes the pawn in front of Sente's king.","61":"Sx7f. Sente takes back with the silver.","62":"S-5d. Gote brings a silver to the centre to firm up its defence.","63":"+B-3g. The horse steps back to guard Sente's camp.","64":"R-7b. Gote swings its rook to file 7, aiming at the head of Sente's king.","65":"P*7e. Sente drops a pawn to block the rook's path.","66":"G-4d. Gote lifts a gold.","68":"K-4c. Gote's king climbs to 4c. It is an unusual shape, with the king close to the middle of the board. This king position will matter a lot in the race later on.","69":"G-6g. Sente lifts a gold next to the silver on 5g, building a defensive wall.","71":"N-7g. The knight jumps up, ready to join the attack.","73":"P-6e. Sente pushes its file-6 pawn into Gote's to shake Gote's camp.","75":"P*5f. Sente drops a pawn to firm up the centre.","76":"P-3f. Gote pushes its file-3 pawn and pokes at Sente's horse.","77":"+Bx3f. The horse takes the pawn.","78":"Px5f. Gote's pawn takes Sente's pawn and presses into the centre.","79":"Sx5f. Sente takes back with the silver.","80":"P*5e. Gote drops a pawn to block the silver's path.","81":"Sx6e. The silver takes the pawn on 6e and approaches Gote's camp from the side.","82":"P*6d. Gote drops a pawn to chase the silver.","83":"Sx5d. The silver takes the silver on 5d, removing one of Gote's defenders.","84":"Gx5d. Gote takes back with the gold.","85":"P*6e. Sente drops another pawn and keeps attacking on file 6.","86":"P*3e. Gote drops a pawn to chase Sente's horse.","87":"+B-3g. The horse goes back to 3g. It is an important defender of Sente's camp, so Sente does not force it forward.","88":"S-4d. Gote lifts a silver to guard the king.","89":"P*5f. Sente drops a pawn into Gote's pawn on 5e, trying to pry open the centre.","91":"Px6d. The pawn on 6e takes the pawn on 6d. A Sente pawn has made its way into Gote's camp.","92":"P-4f. Gote pushes its file-4 pawn and strikes back.","95":"P-6c+. The pawn promotes into a tokin. A tokin moves like a gold, yet if it is captured the opponent gets only a pawn.","96":"Gx6c. Gote takes the tokin with the gold.","97":"P*6f. Sente drops a pawn to fill a gap in its own camp.","98":"N-3c. Gote jumps its knight up to help defend.","99":"S*6e. Sente drops a silver from its hand. This silver is aiming at the gold on 5d.","100":"P*6d. Gote drops a pawn to try to stop the silver.","101":"Sx5d. The silver takes the gold on 5d. Another defender of Gote's king is gone.","102":"Gx5d. Gote takes back with the gold.","103":"P*3d. Sente drops a pawn and pokes at the area near Gote's king.","104":"Kx3d. Gote's king takes the pawn and steps out to 3d. The king is coming further forward.","105":"P*3f. Sente drops a pawn to hold down the front of Gote's king.","106":"S*4d. Gote drops a silver to protect the king.","107":"R-3i. Sente swings the rook back to file 3. Gote's king is on file 3, so the Side Rook is back at work.","108":"K-4c. Gote's king steps back to 4c.","109":"Px3e. The pawn on 3f takes the pawn on 3e. A Sente pawn moves right up close to Gote's king.","110":"P*3h. Gote drops a pawn that attacks the rook on 3i.","111":"G*3d, check. Sente drops a gold from its hand and drives Gote's king further.","112":"K-5c. Gote's king escapes to 5c.","113":"R-5i. Attacked by the pawn on file 3, the rook moves away to 5i.","115":"+B-2f. The horse steps aside to 2f. If Gote's silver came to 4f, it would hit the horse on 3g, so this seems to be dodging in advance.","116":"Sx4f. Gote's silver takes the pawn on 4f and comes closer to Sente's camp.","117":"Gx3c. The gold takes the knight. Sente shaves away Gote's pieces one by one.","119":"G-3d. The gold goes back to 3d, a square that blocks the king's escape.","121":"N*7d. Sente drops a knight. It aims at 8b, where Gote's rook is, and at 6b.","122":"R-4b. Gote runs the rook away to file 4.","123":"P*4d. Sente drops a pawn to pull Gote's defending gold out of place.","124":"Gx4d. Gote takes the pawn with the gold.","125":"Gx4d. Sente takes the gold with its gold.","126":"Rx4d. Gote takes back with the rook.","127":"Gx5f. The gold takes the pawn on 5f.","128":"Sx5f. Gote takes the gold with the silver.","129":"Rx5f. Sente takes back the silver with the rook. The trades go on, and the game reaches its final stage.","130":"S*5e. Gote drops a silver to chase Sente's rook.","131":"R-5i. The rook steps back.","132":"P*5h. Gote drops a pawn to chase the rook again.","133":"R-6i. The rook goes to 6i. Gote keeps chasing Sente's rook with pawns.","134":"K-5d. Gote's king comes out all the way to 5d. A king in the middle of the board has few defenders around it and is easy to target.","135":"+B-3f, check. The horse moves to 3f and gives check to the king on 5d along the diagonal through 4e. The horse that had guarded Sente's camp all this time joins the attack here.","136":"G*4e. Gote drops a gold to block the horse's line.","137":"G*6e, check. Sente drops a gold and closes in from right beside the king.","138":"Px6e. Gote takes the gold with a pawn.","139":"Sx6e, check. The silver on 7f takes that pawn with check. The silver that had stood in front of the king since the middle game joins the attack.","140":"K-4c. Gote's king goes back to 4c.","141":"+Bx4e. The horse takes the gold on 4e. Sente keeps taking the pieces around Gote's king.","142":"Rx4e. Gote takes the horse with the rook.","143":"S*5d, check. Seeing that the king could no longer escape, Sekine admitted defeat here. Sakata wins in 143 moves. He attacked on file 3 with the Side Rook, defended his camp with the horse through a long middle game while shaving away Gote's pieces little by little, and at the end caught Gote's king after it had come forward. It is a stubborn, determined game, just like the self-taught Sakata. After his death, Sakata was given the titles of Meijin and Ōshō."}}};
